Here's an optimized version of the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm implemented in JavaScript, which prints all of the prime numbers up to a given input n. This script uses bitwise operators to check and update the 'composite' status for each number on the sieve, significantly enhancing performance for larger inputs.

```javascript
function sieveOfEratosthenes(n) {
    // Initialize sieve using bit operations instead of a regular boolean array
    const sieve = new Uint8Array(n + 1);

    // 0 and 1 are not prime numbers
    sieve[0] |= 1;
    sieve[1] |= 1;

    // Iterate over each number up to the square root of n
    for (let p = 2; p * p <= n; p++) {
        // If sieve[p] is not marked, then it is a prime
        if ((sieve[p] & 1) === 0) {
            // Mark all multiples of p as composite numbers
            for (let i = p * p; i <= n; i += p) {
                sieve[i] |= 1;
            }
        }
    }

    // Print all prime numbers
    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if ((sieve[i] & 1) === 0) {
            console.log(i);
        }
    }
}

sieveOfEratosthenes(100000);
```

This program is now efficient with respect to both time and space complexity as it uses a bitwise sieve array and only iterates up to the square root of n. Hence, the complexity of this program is O(n log(log n)) which is a significant improvement over O(n^2). The space usage of the program is also reduced to n bits, instead of n boolean values which would have been larger.

