Below is a simple implementation for a real-time clock using HTML and JavaScript:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Real-Time Clock</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Real-Time Clock</h1>
  <p id="time"></p>

  <script type="text/javascript">
  function showTime(){
    var date = new Date();
    var h = date.getHours(); // 0 - 23
    var m = date.getMinutes(); // 0 - 59
    var s = date.getSeconds(); // 0 - 59

    if(h == 0){
      h = 12;
    }

    if(h > 12){
      h = h - 12;
    }

    if(h < 10){
      h = "0" + h;
    }

    if(m < 10){
      m = "0" + m;
    }

    if(s < 10){
      s = "0" + s;
    }

    var time = h + ":" + m + ":" + s;
    document.getElementById("time").innerText = time;
    document.getElementById("time").textContent = time;

    setTimeout(showTime, 1000);

  }

  showTime();
  </script>

</body>
</html>
```
This simple webpage first defines an area (`<p id="time"></p>`) where the time will be displayed. The JavaScript function `showTime()` is then used to get the current date and time, format it as needed, and then place the time in the `display` area. This function is consistently called every second (as `setTimeout(showTime, 1000)`) to update the time displayed on the page. 

Remember, this is a 12-hour format clock. You can convert it to a 24-hour format clock by removing the if condition blocking `if(h > 12){ h = h - 12;}`.

And don't forget, for best practices, CSS should be separated into another file for styling the clock appearance. External JavaScript file is also a great option when the JavaScript code is getting bigger.

