```javascript
// Definitions for complex numbers are of the form { real: num, imag: num }
function complexOperation(cnum1, cnum2, operator) {
    var result = { real: 0, imag: 0 };

    switch (operator) {
    case "+": // Addition
        result.real = cnum1.real + cnum2.real;
        result.imag = cnum1.imag + cnum2.imag;
        break;
    case "-": // Subtraction
        result.real = cnum1.real - cnum2.real;
        result.imag = cnum1.imag - cnum2.imag;
        break;
    case "*": // Multiplication
        result.real = cnum1.real * cnum2.real - cnum1.imag * cnum2.imag;
        result.imag = cnum1.real * cnum2.imag + cnum1.imag * cnum2.real;
        break;
    case "/": // Division
        var denominator = cnum2.real * cnum2.real + cnum2.imag * cnum2.imag;
        if (denominator == 0) {
            return "Error: Attempt to divide by zero";
        } else {
            result.real = (cnum1.real * cnum2.real + cnum1.imag * cnum2.imag) / denominator;
            result.imag = (cnum1.imag * cnum2.real - cnum1.real * cnum2.imag) / denominator;
        }
        break;
    default:
        return "Error: Unknown operator " + operator;
    }

    return result;
}
```

You can use this function like so:

```javascript
complexOperation({ real: 1, imag: 2 }, { real: 3, imag: 4 }, "+") // { real: 4, imag: 6 }
complexOperation({ real: 1, imag: 2 }, { real: 3, imag: 4 }, "-") // { real: -2, imag: -2 }
complexOperation({ real: 1, imag: 2 }, { real: 3, imag: 4 }, "*") // { real: -5, imag: 10 }
complexOperation({ real: 1, imag: 2 }, { real: 3, imag: 4 }, "/") // { real: 0.44, imag: 0.08 }
```

This function uses the rules of complex number arithmetic to perform each operation. The "+", "-", "*" cases are straightforward multiplications and additions. The division case is a bit more complex: it multiplies the numerator and the conjugate of the denominator, then divides by the length of the original denominator. A check is also added to prevent division by zero in the denominator. Any operator other than "+", "-", "*", "/" would return an error message.

