class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Get local timestamp
        DateTime localTimestamp = DateTime.Now;

        // Get UTC timestamp
        DateTime utcTimestamp = DateTime.UtcNow;

        Console.WriteLine("Local timestamp: " + localTimestamp);
        Console.WriteLine("UTC timestamp: " + utcTimestamp);
    }
}
```
This program creates two DateTime objects (localTimestamp and utcTimestamp), which hold the current local and UTC timestamps, respectively. Then it prints these timestamps to the console.

Keep in mind, DateTime.Now and DateTime.UtcNow are properties, not methods. They don't actually "do" anything in terms of computation, meaning they are quite efficient. Their values are updated continually by the .NET runtime, so the values you get are the most contemporaneous.

Furthermore, calling these properties is thread-safe, because no matter how many threads you have, the values they return won't interfere with each other.

Lastly, if you require a higher resolution timestamp, you can use the Stopwatch class which provides a set of methods and properties that can be used to accurately measure elapsed time.

