A problem such as this is relatively complex and requires a detailed solution. I've written a python program that implements the requirements. This solution uses classes to define nodes and the tree structure. It focuses on a binary tree structure, but it can be easily updated to support N-ary trees by changing the children property from a list of two items to a list of N items.

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
        self.data = data
        self.children = [None] * 4  # For Quaternary tree

class Tree:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = None
        self.nodes = {}

    def insert(self, data, parent=None):
        node = Node(data)
        if data in self.nodes:
            raise Exception('Duplicate values are not supported.')

        if not parent:
            self.root = node
        else:
            parentNode = self.nodes.get(parent)
            if parentNode is None:
                raise Exception('Parent node not found.')
            if all(child != None for child in parentNode.children):
                raise Exception('Parent has already four children.')
            for i in range(4):
                if parentNode.children[i] == None:
                    parentNode.children[i] = node
                    break
        self.nodes[data] = node

    def delete(self, data):
        if data not in self.nodes:
            raise Exception('Node to be deleted not found.')
        del self.nodes[data]

    def retrieve(self, data):
        return self.nodes.get(data)

    def spiral_traversal(self, node, level, res):
        if node == None:
            return
        if len(res) < level + 1:
            res.append([])

        res[level].append(node.data)

        for i in range(4):
            self.spiral_traversal(node.children[i], level + 1, res)

    def print_spiral(self, node):
        res = []
        self.spiral_traversal(node, 0, res)

        flag = False
        for i in range(len(res)):
            if flag:
                res[i] = res[i][::-1]
            flag = not flag
            print(res[i])
```

The usage of the above tree structure would go like this:

```python
tree = Tree()
tree.insert(10)
tree.insert(12, 10)
tree.insert(13, 10)
tree.insert(14, 10)
tree.insert(15, 10)
tree.print_spiral(tree.root)
```

Remember that deletion will not automatically re-assign children nodes and that the spiral traversal is always started from the root node. This code also doesn't handle retrieval of nodes by any other method than direct data input. Adding different traversal methods for that would require additional functions. Complexity of the methods in this class are kept minimal via appropriate data structures. All methods are O(1) except for the spiral traversal, which is O(n), with n being total nodes in the tree. Modifying this to support different (like N-ary trees) should involve changing the children data structure and the related methods for managing children.

