In this erroneous version of the function, the main issue is that in the line `subArray.push(primes[i]);`, the coder intends to push a random prime number to the subarray but accidentally uses the value `i` from the outmost loop instead of `randomIndex`. As a result, instead of adding a random prime number, the function ends up trying to add the prime number at the i-th location in the array. This not only causes the same number to be added multiple times, but also eventually results in an attempt to add `undefined` from empty array. To fix the issue, this line should be updated as `subArray.push(primes[randomIndex]);`.

Please find the suggestion for the given task. There are various ways to develop a multithread-safe method that takes a string as input and removes all punctuation marks from it. But here, I'm providing a simple one.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class PunctuationRemover {

    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public String removePunctuation(String source) {
        if (source == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input string cannot be null.");
        }

        lock.lock();

        try {
            return source.replaceAll("[\\p{P}\\p{S}]", "");
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

This `PunctuationRemover` class has a method named `removePunctuation` which replaces all punctuation in the input string based on Unicode property. This method is thread safe because it locks the critical line of code while it is being executed, it means, at any given point of time, only one thread will be able to execute that part of code which is inside the try block. So, that block becomes atomic operation thereby preventing any kind of dirty read.

I have used `\\p{P}` and `\\p{S}` to match punctuation and symbols. `\\p{P}` matches any punctuation character and `\\p{S}` matches any symbol character.

In the case of `NullPointerException`, it would be handled by throwing an `IllegalArgumentException`.

Remember, this code does not account for localized punctuation marks used in non-English languages. If you need to handle punctuation from other languages, you may need to add those character(s) into the regular expression manually. 

Also note that, in the final version you may want to handle the `IllegalArgumentException` in a more user-friendly way rather than letting it propagate up the call stack.

Sure, here is a Python solution. Let's use the basic idea of binary search but modify it slightly for the 2D matrix. When searching a sorted 1D list, binary search in essence halves the search space at every step by comparing the target value with the midpoint of the search space. If the midpoint is less than the target, the first half of the list can be eliminated. If the midpoint is larger than the target, the second half of the list can be ignored.

With a 2D matrix, we can implement a similar strategy. Rather than halving a list, we'll halve the matrix into quadrants at each step.

Let's assume that our matrix is m x n:

```python
def searchMatrix(matrix, target):
    def search_rec(left, up, right, down):
        # If the sub-matrix is invalid, stop recursion.
        if left > right or up > down:
            return False
        # If target is larger than the largest element or smaller than the smallest element in the sub-matrix, stop recursion.
        elif target < matrix[up][left] or target > matrix[down][right]:
            return False

        mid = left + (right - left) // 2

        # Locate 'row' such that matrix[row-1][mid] < target < matrix[row][mid]
        row = up
        while row <= down and matrix[row][mid] <= target:
            if matrix[row][mid] == target:
                return True
            row += 1

        return search_rec(left, row, mid - 1, down) or search_rec(mid + 1, up, right, row - 1)

    return search_rec(0, 0, len(matrix[0]) - 1, len(matrix) - 1)
```

In this code `search_rec()` is a recursive function that takes four arguments: the current left, right, up, and down bounds for the search. At each recursion, it first checks if the target is within the smallest and largest elements in the current sub-matrix. If not, the function returns False, terminating the search. If the target is within bounds, it seeks the "splitting row" (all elements in this row are less than or equal to the target and all elements in the next row are larger than the target). The function then calls itself on the upper-left and lower-right sub-matrices.

The run-time complexity of this algorithm is O(log(mn)) because it "halves" the search space at every recursion. Hence it is very efficient.

