  public static void main(String[] args) {

    // Your test code will go here.

  }
}
```

2. In your class, write a static method for the binary search. Let's say we're searching through an array of integers. Your method might accept a sorted integer array and a target integer to find. Implement the binary search algorithm:

```java
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
  int start = 0;
  int end = arr.length - 1;

  while (start <= end) {
    int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;

    if (arr[mid] == target) {
      return mid;  // element found
    }

    if (arr[mid] < target) {
      start = mid + 1;
    } else {
      end = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return -1;  // element not found
}
```

This method works by repeatedly dividing the search space in half. If the target is equal to the middle element of the array, you've found your target. If the target is less than the middle element, you know it can't be in the right half of the array and thus eliminate this half from your search. If the target is greater than the middle element, you know it can't be in the left half of the array and thus eliminate this half from your search.

3. Now write some test code in your main method:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
  int[] myArray = {2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72, 91};
  int target = 23;
  int targetIndex = binarySearch(myArray, target);

  if(targetIndex == -1) {
    System.out.println("Element not present in the array");
  } else {
    System.out.println("Element found at index: " + targetIndex);
  }
}
```

In this test, we create a sorted array and then try to find the index of the number 23 in that array using our binary search method.

4. Check for edge cases. Always test your program with various inputs to ensure its working as expected. You can test it with an empty array, an array with one element and searching for an element which does not exist in the array.

Remember this algorithm assumes that your input array is already sorted. If it is not, you'll need to sort it before performing the binary search. It has an average and worst-case performance of O(log n), so it is very efficient.

