Bubble sort should generally be avoided for large arrays as it is not efficient. But if you really need a bubble sort algorithm, consider the following optimized JavaScript code which sorts in descending order and handles text strings. It makes use of short-circuiting to avoid unnecessary checks after the array has already been sorted:

```javascript
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let len = arr.length;
  let swapped;
  do {
    swapped = false;
    for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
      if (String(arr[i]) < String(arr[i + 1])) {
        let tmp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] = tmp;
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
  } while (swapped);
  return arr;
}

console.log(bubbleSort([3, 0, 2, 5, -1, 4, 1]));
console.log(bubbleSort(["apple", "Dog", "cat", "Ball", "elephant", "Ant", "donkey", "Cat"]));
```

In this implementation, during each pass through the array, the adjacent elements are compared and swapped if they are in the wrong order (since we are sorting in descending order, arr[i] should be greater than arr[i+1]). The swapping continues until an entire pass is made through the array without needing to swap any elements, indicating that the array is fully sorted in descending order.

Note: This code use String() function to make sure it compares string with string in order to satisfy "should also handle text strings". It's case-sensitive which means 'A' is less than 'a'. If you want to make it case-insensitive, you may need to apply either toLowerCase() or toUpperCase() function, depending your needs. 

Disclaimer: Bubble sort has a worse-case and average complexity of O(n^n), where n is the number of items being sorted. Most practical sorting algorithms have substantially better worse-case or average complexity, often O(n log n). Even other О(n^2) sorting algorithms, such as insertion sort, tend to have better performance than bubble sort.

