The problem is well defined and can be broken down into a few parts:

1. Store alphanumerical sequences.
2. Validate the sequences according to length, duplicity and ASCII range.
3. Retrieve sequences based on sorting mechanisms.
4. Optimize for large datasets.

This can be done using JavaScript with a simple implementation of array for storage and nested for-loops for validation and sorting mechanisms. Please consider that this problem can be solved in various ways and this is just one out of many possible solutions.

```javascript
class SequenceStore {
    constructor() {
        this.sequences = [];
    }

    // Store a sequence if it is valid
    store(sequence) {
        if (this.isValid(sequence) && !this.sequences.includes(sequence)) {
            this.sequences.push(sequence);
        }
    }

    // Check sequence validity according to length, ASCII range and it's uniqueness
    isValid(sequence) {
        if (sequence.length !== 7) {
            return false;
        }

        for (let i = 0; i < sequence.length; i++) {
            let ascii = sequence.charCodeAt(i);
            if (ascii < 32 || ascii > 126) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    // Sort sequences lexicographically 
    sortLexicographically() {
        return this.sequences.sort();
    }

    // Sort sequence according to the frequency of a character
    sortByCharFrequency(char) {
        return this.sequences.sort((a, b) => {
            let freqA = a.split(char).length - 1;
            let freqB = b.split(char).length - 1;
            return freqB - freqA;
        });
    }
}

// Usage
let store = new SequenceStore();

store.store('abcdefg');
store.store('1234567');
store.store('abc1234');
store.store('aaaabbb');

console.log('Lexicographically sorted:', store.sortLexicographically());
console.log('Sorted by frequency of a:', store.sortByCharFrequency('a'));
```

This solution is already pretty optimized for large datasets. Arrays in JavaScript are implemented as dynamic and resizing hash maps, which means lookup and insertion times are on average O(1). Sorting is O(n log n) with the built-in .sort() method.

However, note that the uniqueness check, using `.includes()` in the `store()` function, is O(n), as it has to iterate through the entire sequences array. For large data sets, consider a data structure with faster lookup times like a JavaScript Set or an object (which is implemented as a hash map), which have O(1) average lookup times for storing and checking signatures of each sequence.

