import numpy as np

def check_descending_order(arr):
    for n in range(arr.shape[0]):
        for i in range(arr.shape[1]):
            for j in range(arr.shape[2]):
                for k in range(arr.shape[3]):
                    for m in range(n+1):
                        for p in range(i+1):
                            for q in range(j+1):
                                for r in range(k+1):
                                    if arr[n][i][j][k] < arr[m][p][q][r]:
                                        return False
    return True
```

This function iterates over all the elements in the 4D array, and for each element, it checks whether all the elements in the array with lower indices have smaller or equal values. If this is not the case for any element, the function immediately returns False. If no such elements are found, the function returns True.

Please note that the runtime of this function is quite high (O(n^8) where n is the maximum number of elements in any dimension). Depending on the size of the array, this function might take a long time to finish. So, this is not recommended for large arrays.

If you are working with extremely big arrays and if you have them sequentially sorted in all dimensions (from all elements in a single cell, up to all elements in all dimensions), it would be better to have a different approach. You can compare an element with its previous element only which decreases the time complexity to O(n^4). If you find any element smaller than its previous in any of these dimensions you can return False else return True.

