Below is an example of a JavaScript function that sorts an array of either numerical or string elements in both ascending and descending order, including handling special cases.

```javascript
function sortArray(arr, asc = true) {
    const filteredArr = arr.filter(e => e !== null && e !== undefined && !Number.isNaN(e));

    const sortedArr = filteredArr.sort(function(a, b){
        if(asc){
            return a > b ? 1 : -1;
        } else {
            return a < b ? 1 : -1;
        }
    });

    return sortedArr;
}
```

In this function, the default order is ascending, but you can make it descending by passing false as the second argument.

Time complexity: 
The time complexity of the filter method is O(n), where n is the length of the original array, as it iterates through all the elements.
The sort method has a time complexity of O(n log n) in the average and worst-case scenarios, as the sort operation operates on a divide-and-conquer strategy and all comparisons are done in pairs.
Therefore, the overall worst-case time complexity of this function would be O(n log n) due to the sort operation, as it dominates the overall computation time compared to filter operation for large size of n.

Space complexity:
The space complexity is also O(n), as the sort operation may need to temporarily store an array containing all elements for some implementations, and the filter method also creates a new array. Therefore, space requirements increase linearly with the size of the input.

Please note that JavaScript built-in sort function automatically handles repetition, so it doesn't require any extra step. And input array containing only string, number, null, undefined and NaN will get sorted using this code.

