Here's a simple JavaScript function to this:

```javascript
function isArrayEqual(a, b) {
    if (a instanceof Array && b instanceof Array) {
        if (a.length != b.length)  // checking array length
            return false;
        for (var i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
            if (!isArrayEqual(a[i], b[i]))  // recursively checking elements
                return false;
        return true;  // if every element was equal
    } else {
        return a === b;  // if not an array, just compare normally
    }
}
```

The `isArrayEqual` function will compare two arrays deeply to see if they're identical in terms of structure, elements and order. It does this by iterating over each element in the arrays and checking if they're equal. If an element is another array, it calls itself recursively to check the inner array. If at any point it finds a mismatch, it returns false. 

But if it manages to check every element in the arrays without finding any mismatches, then it returns true, indicating that the arrays are equal.

You can use it like this:

```javascript
var arr1 = [1, 2, [3, 4, [5, 6]]];
var arr2 = [1, 2, [3, 4, [5, 6]]];

console.log(isArrayEqual(arr1, arr2));  // prints: true
```

This code should work for arrays of any depth and elements, as long as they're not circular (that is, an array shouldn't contain itself either directly or indirectly). If the arrays can be circular, you'd need a more complex approach to avoid getting stuck in infinite loops.

Sure, Below is the JavaScript function that calculates the mean, median, and mode of an array:

```javascript
function calcStats(numArray) {
  const mean = numArray.reduce((accum, curVal) => accum + curVal, 0) / numArray.length;

  const median = (() => {
    const sorted = [...numArray].sort((a, b) => a - b);
    if (sorted.length % 2 === 0)
      return (sorted[sorted.length / 2] + sorted[sorted.length / 2 - 1]) / 2
    return sorted[Math.floor(sorted.length / 2)];
  })();

  const mode = (() => {
    const counts = numArray.reduce((accum, num) => {
      accum[num] = (accum[num] || 0) + 1;
      return accum;
    }, {});
    let maxCount = 0;
    let modes = [];
    for (let num in counts) {
      if (counts[num] > maxCount) {
        modes = [num];
        maxCount = counts[num];
      } else if (counts[num] === maxCount) {
        modes.push(num);
      }
    }
    return modes;
  })();

  return { mean, median, mode };
}
console.log(calcStats([1,2,4,4,5])); 
```
