First of all, a representation of the graph is needed. The graphNodes array in the problem statement does not really represent a graph, it just contains some nodes (or vertices). One of many possible representations for a graph in Java is a Map<Integer, List<Integer>>, where each key-value pair represents a vertex and its adjacency list, respectively. Therefore, it is assumed that the given graph is represented in this way.

Another thing to note is that to detect a cycle during a Depth-First Search (DFS), a "visited" set and a "stack" set are needed. The "visited" set stores all the nodes that have been visited during the DFS, while the "stack" set keeps track of the nodes present in the recursion stack of the DFS.

Here is a Java code implementing a DFS with cycle detection:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    static Map<Integer, List<Integer>> graph = new HashMap<>();
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        graph.put(15, Arrays.asList(20));
        graph.put(20, Arrays.asList(30, 25));
        graph.put(30, Arrays.asList(40));
        graph.put(25, Arrays.asList());
        graph.put(40, Arrays.asList());

        int startNode = 15;
        Set<Integer> visited = new HashSet<>();
        boolean hasCycle = dfs(startNode, visited, new HashSet<>());

        System.out.println("Graph has cycle: " + (hasCycle ? "Yes" : "No"));
    }

    static boolean dfs(int node, Set<Integer> visited, Set<Integer> stack) {
        if (stack.contains(node)) { 
            return true;  
        }
        if (visited.contains(node)) {
            return false;  
        }

        visited.add(node);
        stack.add(node);

        if (graph.get(node) != null) {
            for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
                if (dfs(neighbor, visited, stack)) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }

        stack.remove(node);
        return false;
    }
}
```
This code starts a DFS from a given node (in example it starts from node 15) and returns whether the given graph contains a cycle or not.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(V + E), where V is the number of vertices (nodes) in the graph and E is the number of edges. This is because in the worst-case scenario, each edge and vertex needs to be visited at least once.

