To stack multiple HTML elements, CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) `position` and `z-index` properties are commonly used.

The `position` property is used to define the type of positioning method employed for an element (static, relative, fixed, absolute, or sticky).

The `z-index` property specifies the stack order of an element. An element with greater stack order is always in front of an element with a lower stack order. z-index only works on positioned elements (position: absolute, position: relative, position: fixed, or position: sticky).

Now, let's say you've the HTML structure as follows:

```html
<div id="element1">Element 1</div>
<div id="element2">Element 2</div>
```

You would use CSS to position and stack these elements:

```css
#element1 {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0px;
  top: 0px;
  z-index: 1;  /* Lower value, hence would appear behind element2 */
}

#element2 {
  position: absolute;
  left: 40px;
  top: 40px;
  z-index: 2;  /* Higher value, hence would appear in front of element1 */
}
```

In the above case, Element2 will appear on Element1 because it has a higher z-index.

The essence of the strategy is to control the position and z-index of the elements. The elements with higher z-index values will appear at the forefront or forward-most layer of the webpage. It's worth noting that you should use these properties wisely to avoid potential overlapping and design issues. Too many elements with absolute or fixed positions can make the web layout difficult to manage.

