In the "hexToBin" function, the parseInt function is used to convert the hexadecimal number into a decimal number, where '16' is given as the base of the input number. Then, toString(2) is used on the decimal number to convert it into a binary number where '2' is the base.
The final output is therefore, a binary representation of the input hexadecimal number.
The console.log command is used to print the output.

So, for the input '0F', the output will be '1111'.

Here is a Python solution:

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
        self.data = data
        self.next = None

class LinkedList:
    def __init__(self):
        self.head = None

    def append(self, data):
        new_node = Node(data)
        if not self.head:
            self.head = new_node
            return
        last_node = self.head
        while last_node.next:
            last_node = last_node.next
        last_node.next = new_node

    def prepend(self, data):
        new_node = Node(data)
        new_node.next = self.head
        self.head = new_node

    def delete(self, data):
        if self.head is None:
            return
        if self.head.data == data:
            self.head = self.head.next
            return
        current_node = self.head
        while current_node.next:
            if current_node.next.data == data:
                current_node.next = current_node.next.next
                return
            current_node = current_node.next

    def find(self, data):
        current_node = self.head
        while current_node:
            if current_node.data == data:
                return current_node
            current_node = current_node.next
        return None

    def reverse(self):
        prev_node = None
        current_node = self.head
        while current_node:
            next_node = current_node.next
            current_node.next = prev_node
            prev_node = current_node
            current_node = next_node
        self.head = prev_node
```

Time and Space Complexity Analysis:

- append: O(n) time complexity where n is the number of nodes present in the list as we have to traverse through the entire list to append a new node at the end. It has O(1) space complexity because we use a constant amount of extra space to create new_node.

- prepend: O(1) time complexity as no matter how many nodes are there we just add a new one at the start. It also has O(1) space complexity because we allocate one new node.

- delete: O(n) time complexity because in the worst case we might have to go through all the nodes to find and delete the node. It has O(1) space complexity as no extra space is used.

- find: O(n) time complexity where n is the number of nodes present in the list as in the worst case. We might need to search through the entire list to find the data. The space complexity is O(1) as no extra space is used here as well.

- reverse: O(n) time complexity as we have to traverse through all the nodes to reverse the list. Again, the space complexity is O(1) since we are not using any additional data structures. Notice that the space complexity does not change with the number of inputs. It stays constant. this is why it is O(1).

