This problem needs two separate functionalities. We need to fetch and decode data from a URL, and parse and filter JSON. Firstly, we will make a function for fetching and decoding.

JavaScript:

1. Fetching and decoding:

```javascript
const {promisify} = require('util');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const atob = require('atob');

async function fetchAndDecode(url, secretKey) {
  try {
     const response = await fetch(url);
     if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error('HTTP error ' + response.status);
     }
     let data = await response.text();
     let decodedData = atob(data);
     if (decodedData !== secretKey) {
        console.log('Invalid secret key');
        return null;
     }
     return decodedData;
  } catch(error) {
     console.log('There has been a problem with your fetch operation: ' + error.message);
  }
}
```

We use the Fetch API to fetch the data from the specific url and the inbuild atob function to decode data from Base64 format. We return null if the secret key is not matching with the decoded data.

2. Parsing and filtering JSON:

```javascript
function parseAndFilter(data, filterKey) {
   let parsedData;
   try {
      parsedData = JSON.parse(data);
   } catch(error) {
      console.log('Error while parsing: ' + error.message);
      return null;
   }

   function recursiveFilter(obj){
      let arr = [];
      for(let key in obj){
         if(typeof obj[key] === 'object' && obj[key] !== null){
            arr = arr.concat(recursiveFilter(obj[key]));
         } else if(String(obj[key]).includes(filterKey)){
            arr.push(obj[key]);
         }
      }
      return arr;
   }

   return recursiveFilter(parsedData);
}
```

We use the JavaScript JSON.parse method for parsing JSON. If the parsing fails due to invalid JSON, we print an error message and return null. 

Then we filter the parsed data for the required key using a recursive function, dealing with any structure no matter how nested, and return array of values having the filter key.

In terms of time complexity, the fetchAndDecode method has a complexity of O(n) where n is the length of data. As we are making a single API call to fetch data and using atob method to decode, which are O(n) operations. 

The parseAndFilter method also has a complexity of O(n) where n is the number of keys in our data. Parsing JSON is O(n) and our recursive filter function will visit each key only once which makes it O(n). 

Space complexity of both functions is also O(n) as we are storing fetched/decoded and parsed/filtered data into variables. We are also using recursive calls in the parseAndFilter method which adds to the space complexity.

