Here's a simple C# program that calculates the volume of a pyramid. 

The formula to calculate the volume of a pyramid is V = 1 / 3 * base * height

```C#
using System;

public class Program
{
	public static void Main()
	{
		double pyramidBase;
		double pyramidHeight;

		Console.WriteLine("Enter the length of the base of the pyramid:");
		pyramidBase = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());

		Console.WriteLine("Enter the height of the pyramid:");
		pyramidHeight = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());

		double volume = CalculatePyramidVolume(pyramidBase, pyramidHeight);

		Console.WriteLine("The volume of the pyramid is: " + volume);
	}

	public static double CalculatePyramidVolume(double pyramidBase, double pyramidHeight)
	{
		// V = 1 / 3 * base * height
		return (1.0 / 3.0) * pyramidBase * pyramidHeight;
	}
}
```
When you run this program, you'll be prompted to enter the length of the base and the height of the pyramid. After you enter these values, the program will calculate and display the volume of the pyramid.

You can solve this problem in the following way:

Step 1: Calculate the length of the linked list and decrease `k` by `k%length` to remove unnecessary rotations.

Step 2: Determine the pointer changes based on the result of step 1. 

Step 3: Implement the pointer changes to perform the rotation.

Here is the Java solution:

```java
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode next;
 *     ListNode(int x) { val = x; }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null || k == 0) return head;

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;

        ListNode fast = dummy;
        ListNode slow = dummy;

        int length;
        // Find the length of the list
        for (length = 0; fast.next != null; length++) {
            fast = fast.next;
        }

        // Find the node before the rotation point
        for (int j = length - k % length; j > 0; j--) {
            slow = slow.next;
        }

        // Connect the end of the list to the front, break the list at the rotation point and return the new head
        fast.next = dummy.next;
        dummy.next = slow.next;
        slow.next = null;

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

Firstly, this solution gets the length of linked list. Then, it finds the point before the rotation point to divide the list into two parts. After that, it connects the end of the list to the head of the list to make the list circular. Finally, it breaks the list at the rotation point. 

It takes O(n) time complexity as it has to iterate through the list at least once, and O(1) space complexity as it uses only a constant amount of space to store pointers and temporary variables.

Below is a simple implementation of a Trie data structure in Java. This structure can be used to optimize operations such as search, insert, and delete on a collection of strings sharing common prefixes.

```java
import java.util.*;

class TrieNode {
    private Map<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
    private String endOfString = null;

    public Map<Character, TrieNode> getChildren() {
        return children;
    }

    public String getEndOfString() {
        return endOfString;
    }

    public void setEndOfString(String endOfString) {
        this.endOfString = endOfString;
    }
}

class Trie {
    private TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

    public void insert(String word) {
        TrieNode current = root;
        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            current = current.getChildren().computeIfAbsent(word.charAt(i), c -> new TrieNode());
        }
        current.setEndOfString(word);
    }

    public boolean search(String word) {
        TrieNode current = root;
        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            char ch = word.charAt(i);
            TrieNode node = current.getChildren().get(ch);
            if (node == null) {
                return false;
            }
            current = node;
        }
        return current.getEndOfString() != null;
    }

    public void delete(String word) {
        delete(root, word, 0);
    }

    private boolean delete(TrieNode current, String word, int index) {
        if (index == word.length()) {
            if (current.getEndOfString() == null) {
                return false;
            }
            current.setEndOfString(null);
            return current.getChildren().isEmpty();
        }
        char ch = word.charAt(index);
        TrieNode node = current.getChildren().get(ch);
        if (node == null) {
            return false;
        }
        boolean shouldDeleteCurrentNode = delete(node, word, index + 1) && node.getEndOfString() == null;

        if (shouldDeleteCurrentNode) {
            current.getChildren().remove(ch);
            return current.getChildren().isEmpty();
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```
This Trie data structure supports insert, search, and delete operations. Here's how each operation works:

- `Insertion`: It starts from the root node and for each character in the string, it looks up in the child nodes. If the child node for the current character does not exist, it creates a new node and then traverse down the tree.
- `Search`: It starts from the root node and for each character in the string, it moves down the tree. If at any point, the child node for the current character does not exist or if we have traversed the whole string and the endOfString is still null, we return `false` i.e., the string does not exist in the trie. Otherwise, we return `true`.
- `Deletion`: This is a recursive method that traverses down the tree for each character in the string. If the string does not exist, it returns `false`. If we have traversed the string and the endOfString is not null, we set it to null. If the current node is a leaf node, it also deletes the node, and if as a result of this deletion, the parent node becomes a leaf node then we delete the parent as well.

