This task involves creating a button on a webpage and toggling the background color between light gray and dark gray, while also maintaining the user's color choice via local storage. We can complete this task using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

Firstly, we'll set up the HTML for the toggle button:

```html
<button id="color-toggle-button">Toggle Background Color</button>
```

CSS for the default background color to be light gray:

```css
body {
    background-color: #D3D3D3; /* light gray */
}
```

JavaScript for handling the toggle button as well as storing and retrieving the background color state:

```javascript
var button = document.getElementById('color-toggle-button');
// Retrieve the background color from local storage
var backgroundColor = localStorage.getItem('backgroundColor');

// Check if local storage is not available or if it does not contain the backgroundColor item
if (typeof(Storage) !== 'undefined' && backgroundColor !== null) {
    // Set the background color for the body the same as that retrieved from local storage
    document.body.style.backgroundColor = backgroundColor;
} else {
    // Default color to be light gray if local storage is not available
    document.body.style.backgroundColor = '#D3D3D3';
}

// When the toggle button is clicked
button.addEventListener('click', function () {
    // Switch the background color depending on current color
    if (document.body.style.backgroundColor == 'rgb(211, 211, 211)') {
        document.body.style.backgroundColor = '#808080'; // dark gray
    } else {
        document.body.style.backgroundColor = '#D3D3D3'; // light gray
    }

    // Store the selected background color in local storage
    if (typeof(Storage) !== 'undefined') {
        localStorage.setItem('backgroundColor', document.body.style.backgroundColor);
    }
});
```

This solution includes handling of cases where local storage might not be available. If local storage is not available, the default color will be light gray everytime the page is loaded. Similarly, if we can't find the 'backgroundColor' item in local storage, we set the default color to be light gray. When local storage is available, we save the user's chosen background color so it can be used in the next session. Note that the 'rgb(211, 211, 211)' is the RGB equivalent of the '#D3D3D3' hex color.

Here's a simple implementation in JavaScript and HTML to meet the requirements:

HTML part:

```html
<div id="myDiv" style="display:none; background-color:red;">Default Content</div>
<button id="toggleButton">Toggle Visibility</button>
<button id="editButton">Edit Content</button>
<input id="inputField" type="text">
```

JavaScript part:

```javascript
var toggleCount = 0;
var originalState = {
    content: 'Default Content',
    visibility: 'none',
    color: 'red'
};

let myDiv = document.getElementById('myDiv');
let toggleButton = document.getElementById('toggleButton');
let editButton = document.getElementById('editButton');
let inputField = document.getElementById('inputField');

toggleButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
    toggleDiv();
});

editButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
    editContent();
});

function toggleDiv() {
    if (myDiv.style.display === 'none') {
        myDiv.style.display = 'block';
        changeColor();
    } else {
        myDiv.style.display = 'none';
        saveChanges();
    }
    toggleCount++;
    if (toggleCount === 5) {
       resetDiv();
       toggleCount = 0;
    }
}

function changeColor() {
    var randomColor = '#' + Math.floor(Math.random() * 16777215).toString(16);
    myDiv.style.backgroundColor = randomColor;
}

function editContent() {
    if (myDiv.style.display === 'none') {
        alert('Please make the div visible before editing');
    } else {
        myDiv.textContent = inputField.value;
    }
}

function saveChanges() {
    console.log('Content saved: ' + myDiv.textContent);
}

function resetDiv() {
    myDiv.textContent = originalState.content;
    myDiv.style.display = originalState.visibility;
    myDiv.style.backgroundColor = originalState.color;
}
```

This code creates a single div element that can have its visibility and content toggled, and it adds a button to toggle visibility, and another button to edit the content. Each time the visibility is toggled the color of the div changes randomly.

In terms of performance optimization, this code is already fairly performant because the event listeners are being added directly to the elements they affect, rather than every div on the page. However, if there were multiple elements to control and if they are manipulated frequently, you should consider storing elements in variable and using event delegation.

