In finance, the Macaulay duration of a bond is used to measure the bond's sensitivity to interest rate changes. It assists in comparing the response of different bonds to rate changes. Here is a basic implementation of a Java program for calculating Macaulay duration.

```Java
public class MacaulayDuration {

    /**
     * Calculate Macaulay Duration
     * @param cashFlows - An array of cash flows
     * @param ytm - Yield to maturity
     * @return The Macaulay Duration
     */
    static double calcMacaulayDuration(double[] cashFlows, double ytm) {
        double duration = 0.0;
        double totalPV = 0.0;
        //adjust ytm for semi-annual compounding
        ytm = ytm / 2;

        for(int i = 0; i < cashFlows.length; i++) {
            double pv = cashFlows[i] / Math.pow((1 + ytm), i);
            duration += i * pv;
            totalPV += pv;
        }

        return duration / totalPV;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        //example bond with 3 year maturity, 5% annual coupon, and face value of 100
        double[] cashFlows = {5, 5, 105}; 
        //yield to maturity as a decimal (8%)
        double ytm = 0.08; // 

        System.out.println("The Macaulay Duration of the bond is: " + calcMacaulayDuration(cashFlows, ytm));

    }

}
```
This Java program has a class `MacaulayDuration` that calculates the Macaulay duration of a bond. It first calculates the present value of each cash flow, multiplies it by the time it was received, and adds these up. It also calculates the total present value of the cash flows. The duration is then the total present value accounted for at each time, divided by the total present value.

In the main method, we set example values for a bond with a 3 year maturity, a 5% annual coupon, and a face value of 100. The yield to maturity is set to 8%.

Please note that this code is simplified and assumes that every cash flow occurs annually. This is not always the case in the real-world scenarios where we often receive payments semi-annually. To more accurately calculate the Macaulay duration in those cases, you would need to multiply the denominator of your exponent by the number of periods over which payments are made.

