Devise a Java software application that not only takes in two arbitrary sequences of characters and yields the count of characters in the string with the greatest length, but also identify the distinct characters in both strings. If the string lengths are the same, provide the combined total count of distinct characters from both strings.
String distinctCharacterSequence1 = "Hello";
String distinctCharacterSequence2 = "World!";

output:
Here is a simple Java program solution for the problem:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String distinctCharacterSequence1 = "Hello";
        String distinctCharacterSequence2 = "World!";

        int length1 = distinctCharacterSequence1.length();
        int length2 = distinctCharacterSequence2.length();

        if(length1 > length2 ) {
            System.out.println("The greatest length: " + length1);  
            distinctCharacters(distinctCharacterSequence1);
        } else if(length1 < length2) {
            System.out.println("The greatest length: " + length2);
            distinctCharacters(distinctCharacterSequence2);
        } else {
            System.out.println("The combined total count of distinct characters: " +
                                (distinctCharacters(distinctCharacterSequence1) + 
                                 distinctCharacters(distinctCharacterSequence2)));
        }
    }

    public static int distinctCharacters(String s) {
        Set<Character> charSet = new HashSet<Character>();
        for(char c: s.toCharArray()){
            charSet.add(c);
        }

        System.out.println("Distinct characters: "+charSet);
        return charSet.size();
    }
}
```

This program first checks the length of two strings, and if either one is longer, it will print the length and the distinct characters of the longer string. If the lengths of the two strings are the same, it will print the combined total count of distinct characters from both strings. The `HashSet` used in the function `distinctCharacters(String s)` ensures that each character only counts once, which provides the count of distinct characters.

