The current code stores all the Fibonacci sequence in an array of size (n+1). It's memory inefficient because we only need the last two Fibonacci numbers to generate the next one. 

Here's a more memory efficient way in C++: 

```cpp
#include <iostream> 

using namespace std; 

int main() 
{ 
    unsigned long long n = 50, t1 = 0, t2 = 1, nextTerm = 0; 

    for (unsigned long long i = 1; i <= n; ++i) 
    { 
        if(i == 1)
        { 
            cout << " " << t1; 
            continue; 
        } 
        if(i == 2)
        { 
            cout << t2 << " "; 
            continue; 
        } 
        nextTerm = t1 + t2; 
        t1 = t2; 
        t2 = nextTerm; 

        cout << nextTerm << " "; 
    } 
    return 0; 
} 
```
In this code, I have created two variables `t1` and `t2` to hold the last two numbers of the Fibonacci sequence, and `nextTerm` to hold the next value in the sequence. `t1` is initialized to 0 and `t2` to 1, which are the first two numbers in the Fibonacci sequence.

This version of the code only uses three variables regardless of the length of the sequence we're computing. This makes it much more memory efficient.

Also, the numbers are printed as they are generated, so we don't need to store the whole sequence in memory before starting to print it.

