Here is a C++ program that solves the question. In this program we initialize an empty vector to store all the elements situated in the positions that are multiples of any number from the vector 'n'. We then sort this vector and replace each element in the initial vector 'm' that is situated in a position that is a multiple of any number from the vector 'n', with the sorted element from the newly created vector.

```cpp
#include<vector>
#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;

vector<int> sort_by_nth(vector<int> m, vector<int> n){
    vector<int> multiplePositions;
    for(int i=0; i<m.size(); i++){
        for(int j=0; j<n.size(); j++){
            if((i+1) % n[j] == 0){
                multiplePositions.push_back(m[i]);
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    sort(multiplePositions.begin(), multiplePositions.end());
    int k = 0;
    for(int i=0; i<m.size(); i++){
        for(int j=0; j<n.size(); j++){
            if((i+1) % n[j] == 0){
                m[i] = multiplePositions[k++];
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    return m;
}
```
This program works by using the modulus operator to check if the current position is divisible by any number in vector 'n'. If a position is divisible, it pushes the current element into 'multiplePositions', sorts 'multiplePositions', then replaces the original elements with the sorted ones. The '+1' is needed because vector positions start at 0 in C++, not 1. 

