Here is a Java code snippet that defines a recursive function to generate all prime numbers within a specified interval:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        generatePrimes(2, 50).forEach(System.out::println);
    }

    public static List<Integer> generatePrimes(int start, int end) {
        if (start > end) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        } else {
            List<Integer> primes = generatePrimes(start + 1, end);
            if (isPrime(start)) {
                primes.add(0, start);
            }
            return primes;
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        } else if (num == 2) {
            return true;
        } else if (num % 2 == 0) {
            return false;
        } else {
            for (int i = 3; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i += 2) {
                if (num % i == 0) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            return true;
        }
    }
}
```

The method `generatePrimes(start, end)` calculates all prime numbers within the given interval from `start` to `end`. It is recursive as it calls itself to calculate the remaining primes.

The method `isPrime(num)` checks if `num` is prime. 

This program prints all prime numbers from 2 to 50.

Please note that this solution is not optimal from a performance perspective, because calculating prime numbers is a well-studied problem and more advanced algorithms are available.  Recursive algorithm will also go into stack overflow if range is large. However, it will work for the provided range (2 to 50). Also, recursion is not necessary for this particular problem and it is generally better to use an iterative solution for such problems. This code is only to illustrate how recursion can possibly be used.

