In Java, you can use the Set collection framework to keep track of unique shared characters. A HashSet is an efficient way to achieve this. Here is a sample solution:

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.TreeSet;

public class SharedCharacters{
    public static void main(String[] args){
        String input1 = "abcAbcde";
        String input2 = "defaabcDEFgh";
        String result = computeSharedCharacters(input1, input2);
        System.out.println("Shared Characters : "+ result);
    }

    public static String computeSharedCharacters(String input1, String input2){
        // Convert the inputs to character arrays
        char[] charArrayInput1 = input1.toCharArray();
        char[] charArrayInput2 = input2.toCharArray();

        // Use a HashSet to store unique characters for each input
        Set<Character> set1 = new HashSet<>();
        Set<Character> set2 = new HashSet<>();

        // Add each character to the respective set
        for (char c : charArrayInput1) set1.add(c);
        for (char c : charArrayInput2) set2.add(c);

        // Use retainAll method to keep only elements that exist in both sets
        set1.retainAll(set2);

        // Convert the set to a sorted TreeSet and create the result String
        TreeSet<Character> sortedSet = new TreeSet<>(set1);
        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        for(Character c : sortedSet) result.append(c);

        return result.toString();
    }
}
```
This program gets the shared alphabetic characters from two input strings. It converts each input string to a character array and adds each character to a HashSet, which inherently avoids duplicates. Then it uses the retainAll method on one HashSet that removes all characters not present in the other HashSet, ending with a HashSet containing only the shared characters. The HashSet is converted to a TreeSet, which sorts its elements in their natural ordering, and a StringBuilder is used to create the final result string.

