We will split this problem into two parts and solve it: 

**Part 1.** Functionality for counting duplicates 

Here is a function in JavaScript language that validates if each element within a two-dimensional array is a number. It then identifies and counts the duplicates content of the given element.

```JavaScript
function countDuplicates(array, reqElement) {
    let count = 0;
    let countInEach = [];

    if(!Array.isArray(array) || !Number(reqElement)) {
        return 'Invalid input';
    }

    for(let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
        let local_count = 0;
        if(!Array.isArray(array[i])) {
            return 'Invalid input: only two-dimensional arrays allowed';
        }
        for(let j = 0; j < array[i].length; j++) {
            if(Number(array[i][j]) && array[i][j] == reqElement) {
                count++;
                local_count++;
            } else if (!Number(array[i][j])) {
                return 'Invalid input: only number elements allowed';
            }
        }
        countInEach.push(local_count);
    }
    return{
        total: count,
        inEach: countInEach
    };
}
```

In the countDuplicates function, we first initialize a counts variable to store the count of the required element. We then iterate over the rows and then columns of input array. When we find an element matching with the required element then we increment counts and local_count. The local_count keeps track of the count of required element in the current row and is reset after every row. 

