Here is a JavaScript function which fulfills your requirements:

```javascript
function wordCount(text, wordList) {
    // Input validation
    if(typeof text !== 'string' || !Array.isArray(wordList)){
        throw new Error("Invalid Inputs. First argument should be a string and second argument should be an array.");
    }

    // Initialize an empty map for unique words and their counts
    let uniqueWords = new Map();

    // Initialize count for each specific word in wordList
    let wordListCounts = wordList.reduce((acc, word) => {
        acc[word] = 0;
        return acc;
    }, {});

    // Tokenize the text string and process each word
    let words = text.split(/\W/);
    words.forEach(word => {
        if(!word) return;  // Skip empty strings from the tokens

        // Count the total words
        uniqueWords.set(word, (uniqueWords.get(word) || 0) + 1);

        // Increment the count if it is a specific word
        if(wordList.includes(word))
            wordListCounts[word]++;
    });

    // Total words in the string
    let totalWords = words.length;

    // Number of unique words in the string
    let totalUniqueWords = uniqueWords.size;

    return {
        totalWords,
        totalUniqueWords,
        uniqueWords: Array.from(uniqueWords.keys()),
        wordListCounts
    };
}
```

This `wordCount` function accepts a text string and a list of specific words. It throws an error if the inputs are not a string or a list. It tokenizes the string into words and counts the occurrence of each word. It also checks if the word is in the specified list and updates its count. It finally returns the total word count, count of unique words, the unique words themselves, and the counts of the specific words in the list.

Please note that the word tokenization simply splits the string over all non-word characters. It may not work well with more complex texts containing special characters.

