Here's an implementation of a simple decision tree algorithm in JavaScript to classify the given items:

```javascript
// Decision Tree Class
class TreeNode {
  constructor(attribute, value, label) {
    this.attribute = attribute;
    this.value = value;
    this.label = label;
    this.children = [];
  }

  addChild(child) {
    this.children.push(child);
  }
}

// Classifier Function
const classify = (item, tree) => {
  while (tree.label === undefined) {
    const attributeValue = item[tree.attribute];
    for (const child of tree.children) {
      if (child.value === attributeValue) {
        tree = child; // Move to the next level of the tree
        break;
      }
    }
  }
  return tree.label;
};

// Build the Decision Tree
const root = new TreeNode('shape', null, undefined);
const sphericalNode = new TreeNode(undefined, 'spherical', 'Item 1');
const quadrilateralNode = new TreeNode(undefined, 'quadrilateral', 'Item 2');
const threeSidedNode = new TreeNode(undefined, 'three-sided', 'Item 3');

root.addChild(sphericalNode);
root.addChild(quadrilateralNode);
root.addChild(threeSidedNode);

// Items to classify
const item1 = { shape: 'spherical', color: 'crimson', size: 5 };
const item2 = { shape: 'quadrilateral', color: 'emerald', size: 10 };
const item3 = { shape: 'three-sided', color: 'scarlet', size: 8 };

// Classify items using the decision tree
console.log(`Item 1 classified as: ${classify(item1, root)}`); // Output: Item 1 classified as: Item 1
console.log(`Item 2 classified as: ${classify(item2, root)}`); // Output: Item 2 classified as: Item 2
console.log(`Item 3 classified as: ${classify(item3, root)}`); // Output: Item 3 classified as: Item 3
```

In this simple example, the decision tree only considers the items' shapes, since this is enough to uniquely identify them. You can, however, construct more complex trees with additional branches to accommodate more items or refine the classification based on other attributes (e.g., color and size).

