One possible refactor could be as follows:

```typescript
import { Directive, ElementRef, OnDestroy, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { interval, Subscription } from 'rxjs';
import { debounce, switchMap, catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appNetworkTraffic]'
})
export class NetworkTrafficDirective implements OnInit, OnDestroy {

	private subscription: Subscription;

	constructor(private el: ElementRef) {
		this.el.nativeElement.style.display = 'block';
	}

	ngOnInit() {
		// Use RxJS to make a network request every second and handle the response.
		this.subscription = interval(1000)
			.pipe(
				switchMap(() => this.fetchData()),
				catchError(error => {
					console.error('Error occurred:', error);
					return [];
				})
			).subscribe(data => this.displayData(data));
	}

	// Perform cleanup.
	ngOnDestroy() {
		if (this.subscription) {
			this.subscription.unsubscribe();
		}
	}

	// Fetch the data.
	private fetchData(): Promise<any[]> {
		return fetch('http://network-traffic.com/api')
			.then(response => response.json());
	}

	// Display the data.
	private displayData(data) {
		this.el.nativeElement.innerHTML = '';
		data.forEach(item => {
			const div = document.createElement('div');
			div.innerHTML = `Source: ${item.source_ip}, Destination: ${item.destination_ip}, Port: ${item.port_number}, Traffic type: ${item.traffic_type}`;
			this.el.nativeElement.appendChild(div);
		});
	}
}
```

There are a few key improvements made in the refactor:

1. It's desirable to limit our network requests. This is achieved by using an RxJS observable with an interval operator so we're not constantly fetching new data.

2. The catchError operator is used to gracefully handle any errors that may occur during the fetch operations.

3. With `Subscription`, the data stream is limited and cleaned up whenever the directive is being destroyed using `ngOnDestroy()`, preventing memory leaks.

Remember this is just a simple example and there could be much more complexities in real-world applications including usage of Angular services for data fetching, dealing with Web Sockets, complex DOM manipulations or even using Angular's Change Detection strategy to optimize further.

