Craft a minimization algorithm in C++ that deals with the reduction of expenditures in a financial planning scenario. Herein, consider a case where we have more than one budget and these budgets are made up of multiple elements. Your task is to minimize the total cost of expenditure in all budgets by trying to reallocate resources without crossing the budget limit. 

To engage the candidate in more analytical thinking, the upper cost boundary for any specific budget is set to 500 units, and the varied elements within the budgets could encompass values such as: [{expenditure: 100, particulars: ManuscriptPrinting}, {expenditure: 250, particulars: ManuscriptEditing}, {expenditure: 75, particulars: CoverDesigning}] and so on. 

In addition, take into account multiple constraints such as (but not limited to): deadlines, prioritization of tasks, and additional charges in case of outsourcing.

Also, provide a piece of flawed code as a starting point to increase misdirection and challenge their knowledge of common coding mistakes.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Expenditure {
public:
    int cost;
    string name;

    Expenditure(int expenditure, string particulars) {
        cost = expenditure;
        name = particulars;
    }
};

int main() {
    int upper_bound = 500;
    vector<Expenditure> budget = {
        Expenditure(100, "ManuscriptPrinting"),
        Expenditure(250, "ManuscriptEditing"),
        Expenditure(75, "CoverDesigning")};

    for(auto i = budget.begin(); i < budget.end(); ++i) {
        if (i->cost > upper_bound) {
            cout << "Expenditure on " << i->name << " exceeds budget limit.";
        } else {
            upper_bound -= i->cost;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This basic algorithm only checks if any particular exceeds the budget limit but doesn't actually try to optimize the expenditure across different tasks. Your job is to modify it to minimize the total expenditure and meet all specified constraints. Make sure your solution takes into consideration different arrangements of task priorities, budget limits and additional charges in case of outsourcing.

output:
The problem with the given code is that it simply subtracts the expenditure from the upper_bound without considering any constraints such as deadlines, prioritization of tasks, and additional charges. These aspects are critical in real-world budget optimization scenarios. The problem can be solved with an adaptation of the Knapsack problem solution, which is a classic optimization problem in Computer Science.

Before implementing that, we'll need to add few things to our `Expenditure` class to accommodate the new constraints, like adding priority, deadline, and outsourcing cost. Due to complexity concern, we will only consider priority level (lower is higher priority), outsourcing cost, and whether it's feasible to outsource. The full solution, unfortunately, is beyond the scope of this text.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Expenditure {
public:
    int cost;
    int outsourcingCost;
    int priority;
    bool canOutsource;
    string name;

    Expenditure(int _cost, string _name, int _priority, int _outsourcingCost, bool _canOutsource) {
        cost = _cost;
        name = _name;
        priority = _priority;
        outsourcingCost = _outsourcingCost;
        canOutsource = _canOutsource;
    }
};

bool comparison(const Expenditure &a, const Expenditure &b) {
    return a.priority < b.priority;
}

void optimizeBudget(vector<Expenditure> &budget, int budgetLimit) {
    sort(budget.begin(), budget.end(), comparison);

    for (Expenditure &exp : budget) {
        if (exp.cost <= budgetLimit) {
            budgetLimit -= exp.cost;
        } else if (exp.canOutsource && exp.outsourcingCost <= budgetLimit) {
            budgetLimit -= exp.outsourcingCost;
            cout << "Outsourced " << exp.name << "\n";
        } else {
            cout << "Unable to afford " << exp.name << "\n";
        }
    }

    if (budgetLimit > 0) {
        cout << "Budget left: " << budgetLimit;
    }
}

int main() {
    vector<Expenditure> budget = {
        Expenditure(100, "ManuscriptPrinting", 1, 90, true),
        Expenditure(250, "ManuscriptEditing", 2, 200, false),
        Expenditure(75, "CoverDesigning", 3, 50, true)
    };

    int budgetLimit = 300;  // Adjust your budget limit here.

    optimizeBudget(budget, budgetLimit);
}
```

In this code, we assign each expenditure with priority, outsourcing cost, and a boolean `canOutsource` to indicate whether it's feasible or not. We then sort the expenditures based on their priority and start deducting cost from the budget. If it's not affordable but can be outsourced at a lower cost, we do that. Finally, it outputs the tasks which couldn't be afforded and the remaining budget if any. 

The solution provided here is a very basic one that assumes tasks are independent of each other and we can either outsource the whole task or none of it, which is rarely the case in real world. A complete solution would involve a more complex algorithm and possibly use of integer linear programming.

