Here's the Singleton Class with the synchronized approach for multithreaded environment in Java:

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static volatile Singleton instance = null;

    // private constructor to restrict instantiation of the class from other classes
    private Singleton() { 
        // Preemptively do any heavy processing here
    }

    // public method to return the singleton instance
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                // Double check to make sure 'instance' has not been 
                // initialized while thread was waiting for the lock
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
``` 

In the code above, Singleton object will be created only when it is necessary (lazy initialization). Also, we have minimized the synchronization overhead by checking the instance reference twice (`double-checked locking`). 

First checking is without acquiring the lock, which will improve the performance because acquiring & releasing a lock involves OS interaction which is time-consuming. If the instance is already created, there is no locking required. 

If the instance is found to be null initially, then the second check is done after acquiring the lock to ensure the singleton's invariant. 

Using the keyword 'volatile' ensures that changes made to 'instance' will not be cached locally and will be immediately available to other threads, maintaining the 'happens-before' condition which is necessary for correct synchronization in this context.

