Given a faulty piece of code that is supposed to copy an array into a new array in JavaScript, identify the errors and rectify them. Additionally, explain why shallow copy or deep copy would be suitable in this context. 

Faulty Code: 
```javascript
let array1 = [1,2,3,4];
let array2 = array1;
array2.push(5);
console.log(array1);
console.log(array2);
```
Note that in the above code, when the element is pushed into `array2`, `array1` is also getting updated which is not the expected behavior. Your task is to correct this issue in the code.

output:
The problem here is that when you do `let array2 = array1;` in JavaScript, array2 simply points to the same memory location as array1. Hence, any changes you make through `array2` will also affect `array1`. To achieve the intended behavior, a shallow or deep copy of the array needs to be created. 

A shallow copy creates a new object and copies the non-nested properties of the original object to the new object. A deep copy, on the other hand, creates a new object and copies both nested and non-nested properties of the original object to the new object.

If arrays contain simple data types such as integer, boolean, or string, a shallow copy would be appropriate because there are no nested entities. If there are nested liked lists or objects, a deep copy will be needed. In this case, our array contains integers, so we can make a shallow copy using the `Array.prototype.slice()` method, the spread operator `...`, or `Array.from()`. 

Here's the corrected code: 

Using `slice()`:
```javascript
let array1 = [1,2,3,4];
let array2 = array1.slice();
array2.push(5);
console.log(array1);  // prints: [1, 2, 3, 4]
console.log(array2);  // prints: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

Or, using spread operator:
```javascript
let array1 = [1,2,3,4];
let array2 = [...array1];
array2.push(5);
console.log(array1);  // prints: [1, 2, 3, 4]
console.log(array2);  // prints: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

Or, using `Array.from()`:
```javascript
let array1 = [1,2,3,4];
let array2 = Array.from(array1);
array2.push(5);
console.log(array1);  // prints: [1, 2, 3, 4]
console.log(array2);  // prints: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

