Here is a simple program which illustrates the differences between const, let, and var.

```javascript
// Reassignment
var varVariable = "I can be reassigned";
varVariable = "I have been reassigned";
console.log(varVariable);

let letVariable = "I can also be reassigned";
letVariable = "I have also been reassigned";
console.log(letVariable);

const constVariable = "I cannot be reassigned";
// This will throw an error as const variables cannot be reassigned.
// constVariable = "Trying to reassign";
// console.log(constVariable); 

// Scope
var varVariable = "I am a var and I am globally scoped";
if (true) {
    var varVariable = "I am still globally scoped";
}
console.log(varVariable);
// Output: I am still globally scoped

let letVariable = "I am a let and I am block scoped";
if (true) {
    let letVariable = "I am limited to this block";
    console.log(letVariable);
}
console.log(letVariable);
// Output: I am limited to this block
// Output: I am a let and I am block scoped

const constVariable = "I am a const and I am block scoped";
if (true) {
    const constVariable = "I am limited to this block";
    console.log(constVariable);
}
console.log(constVariable);
// Output: I am limited to this block
// Output: I am a const and I am block scoped

// Hoisting
console.log(varHoisting); // This will output undefined, because the declaration (not initialization) gets hoisted
var varHoisting = "I am declared after console.log";

// console.log(letHoisting); // This will throw an error because let and const declarations do not get hoisted 
let letHoisting = "I am a let variable";

// console.log(constHoisting); // This will throw an error because let and const declarations do not get hoisted
const constHoisting = "I am a const variable";
```

Please note, a const variable can't be re-declared or re-assigned whereas let and var can be. A let variable can't be re-declared but can be re-assigned within its scope. Unlike var and let, a const must be initialized at the time of declaration.

