使用Java 1.8的Lambda表達式以及Optional物件，請重寫以下的程式碼片段，並加入處理可能產生的空值以及除以零情況。

BiFunction<Double, Double, Optional<Double>> bf = new BiFunction<Double, Double, Optional<Double>>() {

   public Optional<Double> apply(Double d1, Double d2) { 
      if (d1 == null || d2 == null) {
         return Optional.empty();
      } else if (d2 == 0) {
         throw new ArithmeticException("Division by zero");
      } else {
         return Optional.of((d1 * d2) / 2);
      }
   }
}

output:
Here is the solution implemented in Java 1.8 using Lambda expressions and Optional:

```java
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.function.BiFunction;

public class Solve {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        BiFunction<Optional<Double>, Optional<Double>, Optional<Double>> bf = 
                (Optional<Double> d1, Optional<Double> d2) -> {
                    if (!d1.isPresent() || !d2.isPresent()) {
                        return Optional.empty();
                    } else if (d2.get() == 0) {
                        throw new ArithmeticException("Division by zero");
                    } else {
                        return Optional.of((d1.get() * d2.get()) / 2);
                    }
                };
    }
}
```

In this solution, `d1` and `d2` are Optional values rather than plain Double values, which helps us handle the null cases gracefully. Also, note that throwing an exception when attempting to divide by zero is indeed correct behavior. However, if you would prefer to return an empty Optional in that case, then you can simply replace the line that throws the exception with `return Optional.empty();`.

