Sure, here is the prototype-based JavaScript class to represent a three-dimensional point in space.

```javascript
function Point(x, y, z) {
  this.x = x;
  this.y = y;
  this.z = z;
}

Point.prototype.distanceFromOrigin = function() {
  return Math.sqrt(this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y + this.z * this.z);
}

Point.prototype.translate = function(dx, dy, dz) {
  return new Point(this.x + dx, this.y + dy, this.z + dz);
}

Point.prototype.equals = function(other) {
  return this.x === other.x && this.y === other.y && this.z === other.z;
}

// Creating a point and showing basic usage
var point1 = new Point(1, 2, 3);
console.log('Distance from origin:', point1.distanceFromOrigin());
var point2 = point1.translate(1, 1, 1);
console.log('Point after translation:', point2);
console.log('Are the two points equal?', point1.equals(point2));
```
In the above code, `Point` is a constructor function that initializes a new point with specified `x`, `y`, and `z` coordinates. The `distanceFromOrigin` method computes the Euclidean distance from the point to the origin. The `translate` method creates a new point that is shifted from the original point by the specified amounts in each direction. The `equals` method checks if two points have the same coordinates.

Please note that JavaScript is class-free, "prototype" programming language. However, with recent updates in ECMAScript 2015 (also known as ES6), you can use class syntactical sugar in JavaScript which will still do things in prototype under the hood. Please use the modern approach if your project permits to do so.

