Sure, first you need to have an understanding of the postfix notation. The operators are after the operands and if there's more than one operation, operations are performed from left to right.

Here's an implementation in C++:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stack>
using namespace std;

int evalPostfix(string expression) {
    stack<int> Stack;
    for(int i = 0; i < expression.length(); i++) {
        if(isdigit(expression[i])) {
            Stack.push(expression[i] - '0'); //minus '0' to convert char digit to int
        } else {
            int op1 = Stack.top();
            Stack.pop();
            int op2 = Stack.top();
            Stack.pop();
            switch(expression[i]) {
                case '+': Stack.push(op2 + op1); break;
                case '-': Stack.push(op2 - op1); break;
                case '*': Stack.push(op2 * op1); break;
                case '/': Stack.push(op2 / op1); break;
            }
        }
    }
    return Stack.top();
}

int main() {
    string expression = "231*+9-";
    cout << evalPostfix(expression);
    return 0;
}
```

Steps for the algorithm:
1. Create an empty stack.
2. Start from the left (0th index) of the expression.
3. If the character is digit, convert it to integer and push it to stack.
4. If the character is an operator, pop two elements from stack, perform operation and push the result back to stack.
5. Continue the process until the expression is fully scan.
6. At the end, the stack will be left with only one integer which results from the calculation of the postfix expression.

