Here is a Java program that converts a string of comma-separated values into an array and sort array by the length of strings in descending order.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String data = "Banana, Apple, Apricot, Raspberry";
        // Remove leading/trailing spaces and split data string into array of strings
        String[] items = data.replaceAll("\\s+","").split(",");
        // Sort the array items by length in descending order
        Arrays.sort(items, new Comparator<String>() {
            @Override
            public int compare(String o1, String o2) {
                return o2.length() - o1.length();
            }
        });
        // Print sorted array items
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(items));
    }
}
```

When you run this program, it will print the following output:

```
[Raspberry, Banana, Apricot, Apple]
```

This shows that the string of comma-separated values has been sorted by the length of the strings in descending order. The string with the most characters is printed first, while the string with the fewest characters is printed last.

You can design a PHP class based on object-oriented programming principles to serve as a blueprint for a student entity. This design will encapsulate the student's first name, last name, chronological age, and GPA. Take a look at the following example:

```php
class Student {

  // private attributes
  private $firstName;
  private $lastName;
  private $age;
  private $gpa;

  // constructor
  public function __construct($firstName, $lastName, $age, $gpa) {
    $this->firstName = $firstName;
    $this->lastName = $lastName;
    $this->age = $age;
    $this->gpa = $gpa;
  }

  // getters
  public function getFirstName() {
    return $this->firstName;
  }

  public function getLastName() {
    return $this->lastName;
  }

  public function getAge() {
    return $this->age;
  }

  public function getGPA() {
    return $this->gpa;
  }

  // setters
  public function setFirstName($firstName) {
    $this->firstName = $firstName;
  }

  public function setLastName($lastName) {
    $this->lastName = $lastName;
  }

  public function setAge($age) {
    if($age > 0)
      $this->age = $age;
    else
      echo "Age must be a positive number.";
  }

  public function setGPA($gpa) {
    if($gpa >= 0.0 && $gpa <= 4.0)
      $this->gpa = $gpa;
    else
      echo "GPA must be between 0.0 and 4.0.";
  }
}
```
In this class, we are using private attributes to ensure that they can only be interacted with through the class methods (Getter and Setter methods), consistent with the encapsulation principle of OOP. Through the setters, we can also include some validation logic to ensure that age is always a positive value and GPA is within a reasonable range.

Here is a Java class ('Octagon') that meets your requirements:

```java
public class Octagon {
    //length of the octagon's side
    private double sideLength;

    //constructor
    public Octagon(double sideLength) {
        this.sideLength = sideLength;
    }

    //method to calculate and return the area of the octagon
    public double getArea() {
        return 2 * (1 + Math.sqrt(2)) * Math.pow(sideLength, 2);
    }

    //getter method for side length
    public double getSideLength() {
        return sideLength;
    }

    //setter method for side length
    public void setSideLength(double sideLength) {
        this.sideLength = sideLength;
    }
}
```

The formula used in the 'getArea()' method for the calculation of the octagon's area is '2 * (1 + sqrt(2)) * sideLength^2'.

The 'Octagon' class has a 'sideLength' attribute representing the length of the octagon's side and implements methods to set and get that side length (setSideLength() and getSideLength()) and a method to find the area of the octagon (getArea()). 

