Here is a Java solution to determine the paramount numerical (maximum) value present within an array of integers and its frequency of appearance:

```java
public class MaximumValueFinder {
    public static void main(String[] args){
        int[] arr = {4, 9, 3, 6, 1, 9, -2, 9};
        findMaxAndFrequency(arr);
    }

    public static void findMaxAndFrequency(int[] arr){
        if(arr.length == 0){
            System.out.println("Empty array, no maximum value or frequency.");
        } 
        else{
            int maxValue = arr[0];
            int frequency = 0;

            for(int i=0; i < arr.length; i++){
                if(arr[i] > maxValue){
                    maxValue = arr[i];
                    frequency = 1;
                }
                else if(arr[i] == maxValue){
                    frequency++;
                }
            }

            System.out.println("Max value is: " + maxValue);
            System.out.println("Frequency is: " + frequency);
        }
    }
}
```

The method findMaxAndFrequency takes an array of integers as an input, checks if the array is empty or not.

If it's empty, it outputs a message indicating the array is empty.

If it's not empty, it assumes the first value is the max value and starts iterating over the array. Whenever it finds a value greater than the current maxValue, it sets that as the new maxValue and resets the frequency to 1.

If it finds a value that is equal to currentValue, it increments the frequency by 1.

In the end, it outputs the max value and the frequency. if there are negative numbers in the array, they are handled by the solution as long as they are not the maximum number. But if the array consists of only negative numbers, then the maximum number (even if it is negative) will be printed, as per the definition of maximum.

