Here is a simple Java program which reverses a given string without using the built-in reverse() function:

```Java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "Hello World";
        String reversed = reverseString(str);
        System.out.println("Reversed String: " + reversed);
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str)
    {
        String reverse = "";
        for(int i = str.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            reverse = reverse + str.charAt(i);
        }
        return reverse;
    }
}
```

In this program, the method 'reverseString' iterates through the input string from end to start by decrementing the loop variable 'i'. For each iteration, it appends the character at the current index 'i' to the 'reverse' string. This effectively reverses the string.

The main method simply creates a string, reverses it by calling 'reverseString', and prints out the reversed string. 

If the input string is "Hello World", running this program will output: "dlroW olleH".

