Here's an example of a multi-step Node.js script that reads a text file, converts the text to uppercase, and then replaces certain words. Finally, it logs the modified text to the console.

```js
// Import necessary libraries
const fs = require('fs');
const readline = require('readline');

// Initialize necessary objects
const inputFilePath = 'input.txt';
const fileStream = fs.createReadStream(inputFilePath);
const rl = readline.createInterface({
  input: fileStream,
});

// Define a list of words to replace
const wordsToReplace = {
  'hello': 'hi',
  'world': 'earth',
};

// Multi-step processing
rl.on('line', (line) => {
  // Step 1: Convert line to uppercase
  let modifiedLine = line.toUpperCase();

  // Step 2: Replace certain words
  for (const word in wordsToReplace) {
    const replacement = wordsToReplace[word];
    const regex = new RegExp('\\b' + word + '\\b', 'gi');
    modifiedLine = modifiedLine.replace(regex, replacement);
  }

  // Output modified line
  console.log(modifiedLine);
});

rl.on('close', () => {
  console.log('Finished processing file.');
});
```

You need to have a file named `input.txt` in the same directory as your script, containing some text you want to process. The script will read each line of the `input.txt` file, convert it to uppercase, and replace the words "hello" with "hi" and "world" with "earth", then log the result. After the text processing is finished, the script will log a message indicating the process is complete.

