The Fibonacci sequence is characterized by the fact that every number after the first two is the sum of the two preceding ones. A C++ algorithm to calculate Fibonacci sequence for the first 'n' numbers would look something like this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// Using 'long long' to handle large values
std::vector<long long> fibonacci(int n) {
    std::vector<long long> fib(n);

    // Starting from zero and one
    fib[0] = 0;
    fib[1] = 1;

    for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
        fib[i] = fib[i - 1] + fib[i - 2];
    }

    return fib;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter the number of terms of Fibonacci sequence to output: ";
    std::cin >> n;

    if(n <= 0){
        std::cout << "Please enter a positive integer.";
    } else if (n == 1) {
        std::cout << "Fibonacci sequence upto " << n << " term:0";
    } else{
        std::vector<long long> fibSequence = fibonacci(n);

        std::cout << "Fibonacci sequence upto " << n << " terms is: ";
        for (const auto &i : fibSequence) {
            std::cout << i << ' ';
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program first asks for the number of terms to output, then checks if the number is positive and if it is greater than 1 (as the first two terms are predefined as 0 and 1). If these conditions are met, it calculates the Fibonacci sequence up to 'n' terms and outputs the result.

Please note that this algorithm uses 'long long' to handle large values and 'std::vector' as a suitable data structure to avoid overflow. However, even 'long long' can overflow if 'n' is too large.

