```

Based on this error-prone code, identify the problems and incorporate the necessary corrections in your final answer. Pay special attention to the handling of dynamic data structures like arrays. Implement the remaining methods for removing, updating, and viewing grades. Also, make sure to manage the memory effectively to avoid leaks or other potential issues.

output:
The code has several issues:

1. Private members: Data members should be private for data encapsulation and only accessed or manipulated through member functions.

2. ''sizeof'' operator in 'addGrade' function does not return the length of the array. We have to manage the size ourselves.

3. Memory Leak: Dynamic memory allocation without proper deletion causing memory leakage.

4. No Copy Constructor & Assignment Operator: Since a pointer is part of the class, the default copy constructor and assignment operator would perform shallow copy, causing serious issues (e.g. double deletes).

Here is a corrected version of the code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Scholar {
  private:
    string forename;
    string surname;
    string department;
    vector<int> academic_grades;

  public:
    // default constructor
    Scholar(string fname = " ", string sname = " ", string dept = " "):
        forename(fname), surname(sname), department(dept) {}

    // getter methods
    string getForename() const { return forename; }
    string getSurname() const { return surname; }
    string getDepartment() const { return department; }
    vector<int> getGrades() const { return academic_grades; }

    // Setter Methods
    void setForename(string fname) { forename = fname; }
    void setSurname(string sname) { surname = sname; }
    void setDepartment(string dept) { department = dept; }

    // Operations on academic grades
    void addGrade(int grade) {
        academic_grades.push_back(grade);
    }

    bool updateGrade(int index, int new_grade) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= academic_grades.size()) {
            return false;
        }
        academic_grades[index] = new_grade;
        return true;
    }

    bool removeGrade(int index) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= academic_grades.size()) {
            return false;
        }

        academic_grades.erase(academic_grades.begin() + index);
        return true;
    }

    void viewGrades() const {
        for (int grade : academic_grades) {
            cout << grade << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
};
```
In this correction, I used a vector for academic_grades instead of a dynamically allocated array. It's recommended to avoid naked new/delete in modern C++, and use appropriate library features instead. And I added getter and setter methods to access and manipulate private data members. I provide methods to add, update, remove and view grades. 

When you add a grade, it is appended to the vector. If you want to update or remove a grade, you need to provide an index (grade position) and new grade for updating. If the index is valid, it updates or removes the grade and return true, otherwise returns false. The viewGrades method displays all the grades of the scholar.

