In MongoDB, the relationship can be maintained in two ways:
1. Embedding documents (nested document): In this case, you can put the 'Environments' document inside the 'Aquatic_Species' document. Ideal when you have one-to-one or one-to-many relationships where "many" objects always appear with/within their parent document.
2. Referencing documents: In this case, you'd store the '_id'(UUID type in our case) of Environments document in the 'Aquatic_Species' document. Best when you have a many-to-many relationship or for large hierarchical tree-like structures.

I choose the second method for this database.

