The representation of your input array, and the requirement to sum up values from nested arrays of any depth, contradicts with the incorrect function's signature which uses a jagged array (array of arrays). A jagged array in C# can't support nesting beyond its defined dimensions.

To handle a nested array of any depth, a better approach would be to use a recursive function. This function would check whether each element is a number or an array, and then either add up the number or recursively operate on the array.

In C# specifically, handling arbitrary levels of array nesting can be complex because of its strong typing system. It is not straightforward to declare a type that represents an "array of integers or other such arrays".

But we can handle this by using the dynamic keyword instead of int or int[] in our function signature, and the GetType().IsArray property to check if the current item is an array or a value.

Here's a solution:

``` C#
public static int SumNestedArray(dynamic nestedArray)
{
    int sum = 0;
    foreach(var item in nestedArray)
    {
        if(item.GetType().IsArray)
            sum += SumNestedArray(item as dynamic[]);
        else
            sum += item;
    }
    return sum;
}

public static void Main(string[] args)
{
    dynamic[] numbers = new dynamic[] { new dynamic[] { 1, new dynamic[] { 2, 3 } }, new dynamic[] { 4, 5, 6 }, 7, 8, 9 };
    Console.WriteLine(SumNestedArray(numbers));
}
```

