Here is a C program that asks for two integer inputs, swaps them and checks for possible overflow errors after the swap.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

void safe_swap(int *a, int *b);

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int x, y;

    // reading user's input
    printf("Enter first variable: ");
    scanf("%d", &x);
    printf("Enter second variable: ");
    scanf("%d", &y);

    // call safe swap function
    safe_swap(&x, &y);

    // print the result
    printf("The swapped variables are %d and %d\n", x, y);

    return 0;
}

// function to swap two integers.
void safe_swap(int *a, int *b){
    // check if the addition of both numbers can cause an overflow
    if((*a > 0 && INT_MAX - *a < *b) || (*a < 0 && INT_MIN - *a > *b)) {
        printf("\nSwap causes overflow, aborting!\n");
        return;
    }
    *a = *a + *b;

    // now check if the subtraction can cause an overflow
    if((*a >= 0 && *a - *b < INT_MIN) || (*a <= 0 && *a - *b > INT_MAX)) {
        printf("\nSwap causes an overflow, aborting!\n");
        *a = *a - *b;
        return;
    }
    *b = *a - *b;

    // finally we have to subtract b from a
    if((*a > 0 && *a - *b < INT_MIN) ||  (*a < 0 && *a - *b > INT_MAX)){
        printf("\nSwap causes an overflow, aborting!\n");
        *a = *a + *b; // back to original value
        *b = *a - *b;
        return;
    }
    *a = *a - *b;
}
```

This program works with integer variables and uses `scanf` to get the inputs. It also uses a safe algorithm for swapping the values which involves checking for possible overflows before each operation.

Please, note that the ability to handle different data types for the input variables would require additional complexity to be added to the code given above, such as switch statements or function overloading to process each different type.

