Write an HTML code snippet to render an h2 heading and a paragraph below it. Your HTML code should include Javascript code that, upon clicking the h2 heading, an AJAX call should be made to fetch JSON data from a provided API endpoint. Then, use this data to change the content of the paragraph. Test your code to ensure it effectively handles any errors that might be encountered from the API call.

For reference, identify and correct the errors in the following HTML and Javascript code:

```html
<html>
  <body>
    <h2 id="heading"> Click Me </h2>
    <p id="para"> I'm a paragraph </p>
    <script>
      document.getElementById('heading').addEventListener('click', fetchApiData());

      function fetchApiData() {
        var xmlhttp = new XMLHttpRequest();
        xmlhttp.open("GET", "https://api.example.com/data", false);
        xmlhttp.send();
        var data = JSON.parse(xmlhttp.responseText);
        document.getElementById('para').innerHTML = data.result;
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

output:
The given code has a couple of issues:

1. In the `addEventListener`, the function `fetchApiData` is invoked immediately due to the trailing parentheses `()`. Instead of invoking the function immediately, you actually want to pass the function reference to `addEventListener`.
2. The XMLHttpRequest is synchronous (`false` in `xmlhttp.open`). Synchronous XMLHttpRequests are considered deprecated because they stop the execution of the JavaScript code until the server gives a response, making the web page unresponsive. Hence, it is better to use asynchronous `true` requests.
3. It lacks error handling. If any error happens on API or parsing data, it will crash.
4. Setting the innerHTML using the response can cause Cross Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if data from an unreliable source is used.

Here is the corrected code:

```html
<html>
  <body>
    <h2 id="heading"> Click Me </h2>
    <p id="para"> I'm a paragraph </p>
    <script>
      document.getElementById('heading').addEventListener('click', fetchApiData);

      function fetchApiData() {
        var xmlhttp = new XMLHttpRequest();
        xmlhttp.onreadystatechange = function() {
          if (xmlhttp.readyState == 4) {
            if (xmlhttp.status == 200) {
              try {
                var data = JSON.parse(xmlhttp.responseText);
                document.getElementById('para').textContent = data.result;
              } catch(e) {
                console.error("Parsing error:", e);
              }
            } else {
              console.error("Request failed with status", xmlhttp.status);
            }
          }
        };
        xmlhttp.open("GET", "https://api.example.com/data", true);
        xmlhttp.send();
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
