Here is a simple implementation of a stack in Java:

```java
public class Stack {
    private Node top;
    private int size;

    private class Node {
        private int data;
        private Node next;
    }

    public Stack() {
        top = null;
        size = 0;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == null);
    }

    public void push(int data) {
        Node oldTop = top;
        top = new Node();
        top.data = data;
        top.next = oldTop;
        size++;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if(isEmpty()) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty!");
        }
        int data = top.data;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return data;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if(isEmpty()) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty!");
        }
        return top.data;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
}
```

This simple stack implementation uses a singly linked list where the `top` of the stack is the head of the list. Each `Node` in the list contains `data` and a link to the `next` node.

The `push` operation creates a new node and puts it on the top of the stack. It increments the size of the stack.

The `pop` operation removes the node from the top of the stack, and returns its data. It decrements the size of the stack.

The `peek` operation simply returns the data from the top of the stack without removing it.

The `isEmpty` method checks if the stack is empty by testing whether `top` is `null`.

The `size` method returns the current size of the stack. It's important to note that size isn't a characteristic inherent to the stack data structure, but it's a useful property for many real-world applications of stacks. This method achieves its constant-time performance by maintaining an accurate count of the number of nodes in the stack, incrementing for each push and decrementing for each pop.

