Here is a C# function named "InsertCharacterAdvanced".

```C#
using System;
using System.Linq;

public class CharacterInserter
{
    public static string InsertCharacterAdvanced(string input, char character, int[] indices)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input) || !input.All(char.IsLetterOrDigit))
        {
            throw new ArgumentException("Input string must be alphanumeric and not be null");
        }

        if (indices.Any(index => index < 0 || index >= input.Length))
        {
            throw new ArgumentException("All indices must be valid for the string length");
        }

        char[] result = new char[input.Length + indices.Length];

        for (int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0; i < result.Length; i++)
        {
            if (j < indices.Length && i == indices[j])
            {
                result[i] = character;
                j++;
            }
            else
            {
                result[i] = input[k];
                k++;
            }
        }

        return new string(result);
    }
}
```

You can use this function like this:

```C#
string input = "HelloWorld";
char character = '*';
int[] indices = {5, 7, 9};

try
{
    Console.WriteLine(CharacterInserter.InsertCharacterAdvanced(input, character, indices));
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + ex.Message);
}
```

This function works by creating a new character array with the length of the input string plus the number of indices. Then it iterates over each position in the resulting array - if the current position is in the indices array, it inserts the character, otherwise it copies the next character from the input string.

It also checks that the input string is alphanumeric, if not it throws an ArgumentException. It has also a validation for index bounds.

