Below is a sample C++ solution for calculating the harmonic mean of a dynamic collection of integers using a linked list.

```C++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Node definition
struct node{
    int data;
    node* link;
};

//Initialization of head as NULL
node* head = NULL;

// Insert function
void insert(int val){
    node* temp = new node;
    temp -> data = val;
    temp -> link = NULL;

    if(head == NULL){
        head = temp;
    }
    else{
        node* ptr;
        ptr = head;    
        while(ptr -> link != NULL){
            ptr = ptr->link;
        }
        ptr->link = temp;
    }
} 

// Function to calculate Harmonic Mean 
double harmonicMean() {
    node* temp = head;

    double sum = 0; 
    int length = 0;

    while (temp != NULL){
        if (temp->data == 0) {
            cout << "Harmonic mean undefined as one element is 0" << endl;
            exit(0);  
        }
        sum = sum + (1.0/temp->data);
        length++;
        temp = temp->link;
    } 
    return length/sum;
}

// Driver code
int main(){
    int size, val;
    cout << "Enter the size of the linked list: ";
    cin >> size;

    cout << "Enter elements: ";
    for(int i=0; i<size; i++){
        cin >> val;
        insert(val);
    }
    cout << "Harmonic Mean is " << harmonicMean() << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, first, we create a singly linked list. For inserting each element, a new node is created and as each node gets created the previous node is linked to the new node. The "insert" function manages the creation and linking of each node in the list.

For calculating the harmonic mean, we traverse the linked list using a pointer and for each data, we find its reciprocal and add it to the sum. If the data is 0, the output message "Harmonic mean undefined as one element is 0" will be printed and the program will terminate. If all elements are successfully traversed, finally the harmonic mean is calculated as the total number of elements divided by the sum of the reciprocals. 

This way we ensure the program accounts for scenarios where the arithmetic mean might be zero (as the harmonic mean will be undefined in that case).

