In C++, a pure virtual function is a virtual function that the base class declares but does not define. A class containing at least one pure virtual function is known as an abstract class. This type of function is declared by assigning 0 in its declaration, as shown below:

```cpp
virtual void functionName() = 0;
```

The primary purpose of pure virtual functions, and subsequently, abstract classes, is to provide a foundation for derived classes. In the base abstract class, these functions are not implemented. Instead, they must be implemented by any non-abstract child classes. This enforces a certain structure for all derived classes.

A typical use case for pure virtual functions is the following:

```cpp
class AbstractClass {
    virtual void function() = 0;    // Pure virtual function
};

class ChildClass : public AbstractClass {
    void function() override {      // Implementing the pure virtual function
        // Implementation details...
    }
};
```

Relating to the topic of polymorphism, pure virtual functions are a valuable tool for achieving runtime polymorphism. A base class pointer can call a function that has been implemented differently in each derived class:

```C++
AbstractClass *pointer = new ChildClass();
pointer->function();   // Will call ChildClass's implementation of function()
```

The above fundamentally allows for different implementations to be switched in and out during runtime, which is the core concept of polymorphism.

In terms of error handling, the C++ programming language enforces that pure virtual functions must be implemented in any non-abstract derived class. If a derived class fails to implement a pure virtual function, attempting to compile the program will result in a compile error. This system-level enforcement ensures that no logic errors regarding the implementation of important functions will occur during runtime.

