You can identify the rendering engine and version of the browser in JavaScript using the `navigator.userAgent` property, which returns the user-agent header sent by the browser to the server. Here is a simple example to correctly distinguish the engine and its version:

```javascript
function getEngineDetails() {
  var agent = navigator.userAgent;
  if (agent.indexOf("Trident") > -1) {
    return "Trident (Internet Explorer) version: " + agent.split("Trident/")[1].split(';')[0];
  } else if (agent.indexOf("Gecko") > -1) {
    return "Gecko (Firefox) version: " + agent.split("rv:")[1].split('.')[0];
  } else if (agent.indexOf("AppleWebKit") > -1) {
    if(agent.indexOf("Chrome") > -1) {
      return "Blink (Chrome) version: " + agent.split(" Chrome/")[1].split('.')[0];
    } else {
      return "WebKit (Safari) version: " + agent.split("Version/")[1].split('.')[0];
    }
  } else if (agent.indexOf("Presto") > -1) {
    return "Presto (Opera) version: " + agent.split("Version/")[1].split('.')[0];
  }
  return "Unknown engine.";
}

console.log(getEngineDetails()); // e.g. outputs: "Blink (Chrome) version: 80"
```
In this snippet, we're looking for the names of various browser engines in the user agent string. If the string contains "Trident", we know that the engine is Internet Explorer. If it contains "Gecko", we know the engine is Firefox. If it contains "AppleWebKit" and "Chrome", we know the engine is Chrome (Blink), and if it contains only "AppleWebKit", it's Safari. If it contains "Presto", it's Opera. Please note that user agent strings can be spoofed by the user or by extensions, so they are not a 100% reliable way to detect the browser type and version.

