In this algorithm, we create two empty arrays, `even` and `odd`. Then, we loop through every integer in the input array. For each integer, we check if it's dividable by 2 (i.e., it's an even number). If it is, we push it into the `even` array; otherwise, we push it into the `odd` array.

Time complexity:
This solution has a time complexity of O(n) because we're performing a single iteration over the array. 'n' here represents the number of elements in the array. Each operation inside the loop (the if check, and the push operation) are constant time operations, and hence the time complexity is linear in nature.

Space complexity:
The space complexity of this solution is also O(n). We're creating two new arrays (even and odd) that will store all elements of the original array. Hence the amount of memory used grows linearly with the size of the input, making the space complexity linear.

