I would like to divide this task into three parts: 

1. Checking if `task_5` element exists in the DOM
2. Modifying the `task_5` element
3. Adding new elements

The corrected code looks like this:

```javascript
function modifyHtml() {
    var element = document.getElementById("task_5");

    // 1. Check if "task_5" element exists
    if (element == null) {
        console.error("Element with id 'task_5' not found!");
        return;
    }

    // 2. Only changing the text content and not the innerHTML
    // To prevent unwanted HTML injection
    element.textContent = "Hello World!";

    // Modify Attribute
    element.setAttribute("class", "modified-task");

    // 3. Add new elements
    var newElement = document.createElement("div");
    newElement.textContent = "This is a new element!";
    newElement.setAttribute("id", "new_Element");

    // Add newElement to the body (or any other container)
    document.body.appendChild(newElement);
}
```

Modification Explanations:

- I first perform a null check to ensure that we have an element with id `task_5`. If such an element does not exist, the function will exit immediately, preventing any null pointer exceptions further down the line.

- I've replaced `innerHTML` with `textContent`. This is because `innerHTML` can cause HTML injection, potentially leading to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if the text being inserted is not strictly controlled. On the other hand, `textContent` will convert everything to text, preventing this issue.

- I'm using `setAttribute` to modify an attribute (in this case, `class`). However, there may be other ways to modify attributes depending on the specific needs, such as `className` for class attribute, or direct modification like `element.id`.

- To add new elements, I create a new element with `document.createElement`, set its text and attributes, and append it to the body. In real situations, you might want to append to other containers, so replace `document.body` as needed.

- For error prevention, besides the `null` check mentioned, I am also assuming `document.body` exists (which is usually a safe assumption). If this cannot be assumed, further checks can be added.

- Regarding performance concerns, this code is quite efficient and does not pose issues for a typical web page. However, if you're making frequent DOM updates or working with a huge DOM tree, you might want to look into optimization strategies such as using DocumentFragment, taking advantage of `requestAnimationFrame`, etc. For most scenarios, though, this code would suffice.

