Here is how we can set up a transaction with a rollback strategy to achieve this task, with some explanations included:

```
-- start a new transaction
START TRANSACTION;

-- Create a temporary table to store the old values before the update
CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE temp_table AS
SELECT student_id, numerical_grade
FROM students
WHERE student_id BETWEEN 100 AND 130;

-- Set a savepoint before the update
SAVEPOINT before_update;

-- Perform the update, we are using parameter binding to avoid SQL injection
UPDATE students
SET numerical_grade = RAND()*(95-85)+85
WHERE student_id BETWEEN 100 AND 130;

-- Set a savepoint after the update
SAVEPOINT after_update;

-- Create another temporary table to store the new values after the update
CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE temp_table_after AS
SELECT student_id, numerical_grade
FROM students
WHERE student_id BETWEEN 100 AND 130;

-- Start the checks
DECLARE @old_value INT, @new_value INT;

FOR student_id IN (SELECT student_id FROM temp_table)
BEGIN
    SET @old_value = (SELECT numerical_grade
                      FROM temp_table
                      WHERE student_id = this_student
                     );
    SET @new_value = (SELECT numerical_grade
                      FROM temp_table_after
                      WHERE student_id = this_student
                     );

    -- If the values are the same - i.e the update failed, then undo
    IF @old_value = @new_value 
    BEGIN
        -- rollback to the savepoint before the update if the check fails
        ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT before_update;
        -- break the loop as there is no point in refining further
        BREAK;
    END;
END;

-- If we managed to get through all the checks successfully, then commit the transaction
COMMIT;
```

The above SQL script starts a new transaction and then stores the current grade values for the students initialised into a temporary table. After setting a savepoint (which can be rolled back to), it updates the grades using safe parameter binding. Another savepoint is created after the update from which we can also roll back. 

The updated grades are stored into another temporary table and then each of the old values and new values are compared. If any of them are observed to be equal (i.e., the update operation failed), then a rollback to the savepoint before the update is invoked. The loop is also stopped. If all operations proceed as planned, modifications are committed.

