The Fibonacci series is a type of integer sequence where every number after the first two (taken as 0 and 1) is the sum of the two preceding ones (0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, and so on). Here's a simple JavaScript function to determine all Fibonacci numbers up to a given limit (n):

```javascript
function fibonacciSeries(n) {
  // initialize the variables
  let fibonacciArr = [0, 1];

  // while the last number + second last number is less than n;
  while ((fibonacciArr[fibonacciArr.length - 1] + fibonacciArr[fibonacciArr.length - 2]) < n) {
    // push the sum of last two numbers to the list
    fibonacciArr.push(fibonacciArr[fibonacciArr.length - 1] + fibonacciArr[fibonacciArr.length - 2]);
  }
  return fibonacciArr;
}

console.log(fibonacciSeries(10)); // to call the function for n=10
```

The function `fibonacciSeries(n)` is called with the parameter value 10. It initializes an array `fibonacciArr` with the first two Fibonacci numbers (0 and 1) and then enters a loop that continues as long as the sum of the last two numbers in the Fibonacci series is less than `n`.

In each loop iteration, it calculates the next Fibonacci number as the sum of the last two and pushes it to the `fibonacciArr`. Once the sum of the last two numbers in the series is greater or equal to `n`, it stops the loop and returns the `fibonacciArr` with all the Fibonacci numbers up to `n`. The result for `n`=10 is `[0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8]`.

