Here is the Java code to calculate the sum of the two series.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter a positive integer:");
        int n = scanner.nextInt();

        long startTime1 = System.nanoTime();
        int sum1 = sumOfCubes(n);
        long endTime1 = System.nanoTime();

        long startTime2 = System.nanoTime();
        int sum2 = sumOfSixth(n);
        long endTime2 = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("Sum of 1^3 + 2^3 +...+ n^3: " + sum1);
        System.out.println("Time complexity of first series calculation: " + (endTime1 - startTime1) + " ns");

        System.out.println("Sum of 1^6 + 2^6 +...+ n^6: " + sum2);
        System.out.println("Time complexity of second series calculation: " + (endTime2 - startTime2) + " ns");
    }

    private static int sumOfCubes(int n) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            sum += Math.pow(i, 3);
        }
        return sum;
    }

    private static int sumOfSixth(int n) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            sum += Math.pow(i, 6);
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```
This program first prompts the user to enter a positive integer. Then it calculates the sum of the two series by calling the functions sumOfCubes(n) and sumOfSixth(n). It also measures the time complexities of these calculations by recording the system's nano time before and after each calculation.

Note that, given a number n, the time complexity of each of these two functions is O(n) because each function contains a loop that runs n times where it performs a constant amount of work.

However, this code would only compute the average time taken for performing these calculations. If you want to get the Big O notation time complexities, you don't need to compute it through code. It can be theoretically determined through analysis where you would find they have time complexities of O(n).

Right now the program naively does the calculations, and might have performance issues for larger n. The operations could be optimized to O(1) if you use the formulas

Sum of cubes = [n*(n+1)/2]^2
Sum of powers of 6 = (n*(n+1)*(2n+1)*(3*n^4 - 3*n^2 - 1))/42
This way for any n, the computation is always constant time.

