Here's a simple implementation of a system that handles user login:

```java
class UserLoginSystem {
    private String correctUserName = "admin";
    private String correctPassword = "password";
    private int loginAttempts = 0;

    public void login(String username, String password) {
        try {
            if (!username.equals(correctUserName) || !password.equals(correctPassword)) {
                loginAttempts++;
                if(loginAttempts >= 3) {
                    throw new MultipleFailedLoginAttemptsException("Too many failed login attempts. Please try again later.");
                }
                throw new InvalidCredentialsException("Username or password is incorrect!");
            }
            System.out.println("Logged in successfully!");
            loginAttempts = 0; // reset login attempts
        } catch (InvalidCredentialsException | MultipleFailedLoginAttemptsException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An Unknown error occurred. Please try again later."); // General system failure
        }
    }
}

class InvalidCredentialsException extends Exception {
    public InvalidCredentialsException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

class MultipleFailedLoginAttemptsException extends Exception {
    public MultipleFailedLoginAttemptsException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

In this code, `UserLoginSystem` is a simple class attempting to simulate the login process of a user. If the username or password provided does not match the expected values, an `InvalidCredentialsException` is thrown and caught, informing the user that their credentials are incorrect.

If there have been three unsuccessful login attempts, a `MultipleFailedLoginAttemptsException` is thrown warning the user that they have had too many failed login attempts.

Exception handling is important in this circumstances for several reasons:

1. It allows you to handle exceptions in an organized manner, rather than having large amounts of error-checking code interspersed with your normal, functional code.

2. It allows you to isolate sections of your code from each other so that if one part fails, it doesn't necessarily mean that the entire program has to shut down.

3. By providing feedback to the user about what went wrong (in a controlled manner), it can help with troubleshooting and provide a better user experience. The user is not left in the dark about what went wrong and why they can't log in.

Note: In real-world applications, never store passwords in plain text as shown in this example. Always hash and salt them for security purposes.

