Here is an example of how to reverse a string in C++ without using the built-in string library function. This program uses a character array to store the string and a simple for loop to reverse it.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void reverseString(char* str) {
    // To find the length of the string
    int len = 0;
    for (len = 0; str[len] != '\0'; len++) {
    }

    // Swapping characters from both ends of the string
    int i = 0, j = len - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        char temp = str[i];
        str[i] = str[j];
        str[j] = temp;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
}

int main() {
    char str[] = "Hello, My name is John!";
    std::cout << "Original String: " << str << std::endl;
    reverseString(str);
    std::cout << "Reversed String: " << str << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we do not use any built-in string functions. We write a function `reverseString` ourselves to reverse a char array which is essentially a string. The program finds the length of the string and swap characters from the start of the string with characters from the end.

This code should work with string of any length. Be aware that the reversed string will overwrite the original string.

