```javascript
function shuffle(array) {
    let copy = [...array];
    for (let i = copy.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
        [copy[i], copy[j]] = [copy[j], copy[i]];
    }
    return copy;
}

let array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7];
console.log(shuffle(array));
```

This function works by making a copy of the input array (to prevent mutation of the original array) and looping through it in reverse order. On each iteration it generates a random index `j` and then swaps the elements at the current index `i` and the randomly generated index `j`. This results in a randomly shuffled new array.

This approach, also known as the Fisher-Yates (or Knuth) shuffle algorithm, ensures an unbiased permutation of the array.

