Creating a multilingual web application that displays "Greetings, Universe!" on the user's detected language is a great project which includes work both on the back-end and on the front-end.

Here is a way to do this using JavaScript, Node.js for the backend and Express.js as a web application framework. For simplicity of the example, we will use only two languages: English and French.

First, let's create our server using Node.js and Express.js:

```javascript
// Importing needed modules
const express = require('express');
const acceptLanguageParser = require('accept-language-parser');

// Creating a new express server
const app = express();

// Defining our default and supported languages
const DEFAULT_LANGUAGE = 'en';
const SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES = ['en', 'fr'];

// Create a middleware to parse the user's language from the request headers
app.use((req, res, next) => {
    const languages = acceptLanguageParser.parse(req.headers['accept-language']);
    for (let i = 0; i < languages.length; i++) {
        if (SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES.includes(languages[i].code)) {
            req.language = languages[i].code;
            return next();
        }
    }
    req.language = DEFAULT_LANGUAGE;
    next();
});

// Serving our multilingual greeting
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    if (req.language === 'fr') {
        res.send('Salutations, Univers!');
    } else {
        res.send('Greetings, Universe!');
    }
});

// Starting the server
app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server listening on http://localhost:3000');
});
```

You can install the `accept-language-parser` package using npm, it parses the accept-language header from an HTTP request and produces an array of languages, ordered by preference.

