In response to this problem, we are using Python, and we will deploy a recursive depth-first search (DFS) algorithm to find a specific node as we traverse the ternary tree. Due to the potential large scale of the tree, to minimize memory usage, a recursive approach is implemented instead of a BFS approach.

For insert and delete operations, a self-balancing function is executed at the end of the process to keep the tree balanced. This can add some complex overhead during these operations but it makes sure that operations like search, insert and delete will always be operated with a time complexity of O(log n).

Please note that Python's built-in garbage collector, which removes unreferenced objects, takes care of memory management.

```python
class Node():
	def __init__(self, value):
		self.value = value
		self.left = self.middle = self.right = None


class TernaryTree:
	def __init__(self):
		self.root = None

	def insert(self,value):
		self.root=self._insert(self.root,value)

	def _insert(self,node,value):
		if node is None:
			node = Node(value)
		else:
			if value < node.value:
				if node.left is None:
					node.left = Node(value)
				else:
					self._insert(node.left, value)
			elif value > node.value:
				if node.right is None:
					node.right = Node(value)
				else:
					self._insert(node.right, value)
			else:
				if node.middle is None:
					node.middle = Node(value)
				else:
					self._insert(node.middle, value)
		self.root = self._balance(node)
		return node

	def _rotate_left(self, node):
		n = node.right
		node.right = n.left
		n.left = node
		return n

	def _rotate_right(self, node):
		n = node.left
		node.left = n.right
		n.right = node
		return n

	def _balance(self, node):
		if self._height(node.left) - self._height(node.right) > 1:
			if self._height(node.left.left) > self._height(node.right):
				node = self._rotate_right(node)
			else:
				node.left = self._rotate_left(node.left)
				node = self._rotate_right(node)
		elif self._height(node.right) - self._height(node.left) > 1:
			if self._height(node.right.right) > self._height(node.left):
				node = self._rotate_left(node)
			else:
				node.right = self._rotate_right(node.right)
				node = self._rotate_left(node)
		return node

	def _height(self, node):
		if node is None:
			return 0
		else:
			return max(self._height(node.left), self._height(node.right)) + 1

	def DepthFirstSearch(self, value):
		return self._DepthFirstSearch(self.root, value)

	def _DepthFirstSearch(self, node, value):
		if node is None:
			return False
		elif node.value == value:
			return True
		else:
			return self._DepthFirstSearch(node.left, value) or \
				        self._DepthFirstSearch(node.middle, value) or \
						self._DepthFirstSearch(node.right, value)
```

This code creates a ternary tree with functions for self-balancing, values insertion and depth-first search. Please note that due to the complexity of the problem, many simplifications were made for this sample code. The deletion of nodes, for example, has been left out. Deleting a node on a self-balancing tree requires special handling, as it may cause imbalances in the tree.

