Here is the Java program, including both exception handling for non-numeric inputs and a function to check if the sum is prime or not:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> numbers = Arrays.asList("1", "2", "3", "a");
        int sum = 0;
        for (String num : numbers) {
            try {
                sum += Math.pow(Integer.parseInt(num), 2);
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.out.println("Encountered a non-numeric input: " + num);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Sum of squares: " + sum);
        if(isPrime(sum)){
            System.out.println(sum + " is a prime number");
        } else {
            System.out.println(sum + " is not a prime number");
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i < Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
In this code, a try-catch block is there to handle the `NumberFormatException` thrown due to non-numeric input. If a non-numeric input is encountered, a meaningful error message is printed, and then processing continues with the next numbers.

The `isPrime` function checks whether a given number is prime or not by checking whether it is divisible by any number up to its square root. If it is divisible by any of these numbers, it is not prime; otherwise, it is prime. This function is called after the sum of squares is calculated, to check if the sum is prime. The result of this check is then printed.

