from collections import defaultdict

def get_triplet_indices(data):
    if not all(isinstance(x, list) for x in data):
        data = [data]
    triplet_dict = defaultdict(list)
    triplet_list = []
    for i, list_ in enumerate(data):
        for j in range(len(list_)):
            start_ndx = j
            ndx_list = [i, start_ndx]
            if j == len(list_) - 2:
                triplet = (list_[j], list_[j+1], data[(i+1)%len(data)][0])
            elif j == len(list_) - 1:
                triplet = (list_[j], data[(i+1)%len(data)][0], data[(i+1)%len(data)][1])
            else:
                triplet = tuple(list_[j: j+3])
            triplet_list.append(triplet)
            triplet_dict[triplet].append(tuple(ndx_list))
    return triplet_list, dict(triplet_dict)
```

This Python function is optimized for time complexity and can handle large data sets efficiently. It begins by converting one-dimensional lists into two-dimensional ones for uniformity. It starts iterating over the lists, creating triplets from sequential elements. When reaching the end of a list, it continues on to the next list (or wraps around to the first list for the last list) to create the final two triplets. It indexes these triplets in the format specified and handles duplicate triplets efficiently by adding their indices to the existing key in the dictionary.

For input data containing complex data structures, Python will consider them individual elements when creating the triplets. Note that these complex elements must be hashable (able to be used as dictionary keys)—for example, nested lists and dictionaries are not by default in Python.

The function returns all the triplets in an array and all the triplets with their indices in a dictionary.

Some assumptions about this problem have been made. It assumes that each inner list in the multi-dimensional array is long enough to create at least one triplet. It also assumes that a "circular" array wraps only between the last list and the first list, not within each list.

