Sure, here is a basic idea of how you can define a function to remove all occurrences of a specific value from a bidirectional doubly-linked list in C++.

Firstly, let's say you start with a Node class definition like this:

```cpp
struct Node{
    int data;
    Node *prev, *next;
    Node(int val): data(val), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
};
```

Now this is the function for removing elements:

```cpp
void removeElement(Node* &head, int val) {
    Node* current = head;
    while(current != nullptr) {
        if(current->data == val) {
            if(current->prev != nullptr)
                current->prev->next = current->next;
            else
                head = current->next;

            if(current->next != nullptr)
                current->next->prev = current->prev;

            Node* temp = current;
            current = current->next;
            delete temp;
        }
        else {
            current = current->next;
        }
    }
}
```

The function basically iterates over the list checking each node. If the value of the node equals 'val', it becomes isolated (the previous and next nodes point to each other instead of it) and then deleted. The iteration continues until reaching a node pointing to nullptr (the end of the list).

Do not forget to use the delete keyword to free up the memory used by the nodes that you are removing. If you don't do this, you will cause a memory leak, which can lead to your program running out of memory.

