This can be achieved using the C++11 std::thread library and std::condition_variable for synchronization. Here is the C++ code for this problem.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

std::mutex mtx;
std::condition_variable cv;
bool odd_turn = true; // odd numbers start first

void print_odd(int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i += 2) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        cv.wait(lock, [] { return odd_turn; }); // wait for odd_turn
        std::cout << "Odd: " << i << std::endl;
        odd_turn = false; // switch to even numbers
        cv.notify_one(); // notify the other thread
    }
}

void print_even(int n) {
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i += 2) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        cv.wait(lock, [] { return !odd_turn; }); // wait for even_turn
        std::cout << "Even: " << i << std::endl;
        odd_turn = true; // switch to odd numbers
        cv.notify_one(); // notify the other thread
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(print_odd, 100);
    std::thread t2(print_even, 100);

    t1.join(); // wait for t1 to finish
    t2.join(); // wait for t2 to finish
    return 0;
}
```

This code creates two threads, one for printing odd numbers and the other for even numbers. Each thread acquires a lock on the mutex and checks if it is its turn. If not, it waits on the condition variable. If it is, it prints the number and toggles the condition variable and notifies the other thread.

A unique lock is used because we need to be able to unlock the mutex in order to wait on the condition variable. The lambda functions that are passed to the `wait` function are called the "predicates". They are checked every time the thread is awakened. If the predicate returns `false`, the thread will wait again. If it returns `true`, the thread will continue to execute. After the number is printed, the condition variable is toggled and the other thread is notified.

Here is a Python solution for your problem. The basic logic is straightforward. Within the range defined by start and end, we go through each number possible. If the number is smaller than 'n', and can be divided by either 9 or 14, we examine the number digit by digit, and count how many times '5' appears.

```python
def five_div_seq(n: int, start: int, end: int) -> int:
    count = 0
    for number in range(start, end-1, -1):
        if number < n and (number % 9 == 0 or number % 14 == 0):
            for digit in str(number):
                if digit == '5':
                    count += 1  
    return count
```

