Write a JavaScript function that sorts an array in descending order. Your code has to solve the problem using a specific sorting algorithm you will hand code and not the built-in sort() method. Additionally, identify and fix a piece of erroneous code given below which is supposed to execute this task. 

Erroneous Code:
```javascript
function sortDescending(arr) {
    for(let i=0; i<arr.length; i++) {
        for(let j=0; j<arr.length; j++) {
            if(arr[i] > arr[j]) {
                let temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return arr;
}

console.log(sortDescending([10,12,2,23,67]));
```
Expected array after sorting in descending order: [67,23,12,10,2]

output:
The issue with the erroneous code is in the if condition, where it should be comparing the next item in the array (j+1) instead of the current item (j), and it should be checking if item j is less than item i. 

Here's a corrected version of that code using Bubble sort:
```javascript
function sortDescending(arr) {
    for(let i=0; i<arr.length; i++) {
        for(let j=0; j<arr.length-i-1; j++) {
            if(arr[j] < arr[j+1]) {
                let temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return arr;
}

console.log(sortDescending([10,12,2,23,67])); // [67,23,12,10,2]
```
This code uses the bubble sort algorithm. In each iteration of the outer loop, the largest value "bubbles" up to its correct position. The 'arr.length-i-1' portion of the inner loop allows us to avoid unnecessary comparisons with elements we know are already in their correct positions.

