Below is a basic code snippet of a ComplexNumber class in Java:

```Java
public class ComplexNumber {
    private double real;
    private double imaginary;

    //Default constructor
    public ComplexNumber() {
        this.real = 0.0;
        this.imaginary = 0.0;
    }

    //Parameterized constructor
    public ComplexNumber(double real, double imaginary) {
        this.real = real;
        this.imaginary = imaginary;
    }

    //Getters and Setters for the real and imaginary parts
    public double getReal() {
        return this.real;
    }

    public void setReal(double real) {
        this.real = real;
    }

    public double getImaginary() {
        return this.imaginary;
    }

    public void setImaginary(double imaginary) {
        this.imaginary = imaginary;
    }

    //Method to add two complex numbers
    public ComplexNumber add(ComplexNumber other) {
        double realPart = this.real + other.getReal();
        double imaginaryPart = this.imaginary + other.getImaginary();
        return new ComplexNumber(realPart, imaginaryPart);
    }

    //Method to subtract two complex numbers
    public ComplexNumber subtract(ComplexNumber other) {
        double realPart = this.real - other.getReal();
        double imaginaryPart = this.imaginary - other.getImaginary();
        return new ComplexNumber(realPart, imaginaryPart);
    }

    //Print Method for displaying the number
    public void print() {
        System.out.println(this.real + " + " + this.imaginary + "i");
    }
}
```

Please note that complex numbers are typically represented in the form "a + bi" where "a" is the real part and "b" is the imaginary part of the complex number. In this class, we can add and subtract complex numbers, and also get and set real and imaginary parts.

