Can you provide an alternative solution which would generate the same results as the subsequent JavaScript code snippet with the additional challenge of excluding certain names from the list?

You have an array of people with their respective occupations, structured as follows:

const people = [
	{ name: "Alice", occupation: "Software Developer" },
	{ name: "Eve", occupation: "Data Scientist" },
	{ name: "Carol", occupation: "Web Designer" },
	{ name: "Bob", occupation: "Quality Analyst" }
];

Currently, the code prints all the names in the array:

people.forEach(elementaryParticipant => {
    console.log(elementaryParticipant.name);
});

Your task is to provide an alternative solution where it would generate the same results as the previous snippet but with the added requirement to exclude certain occupations (for example, "Data Scientist") from being printed. Implement efficient searching and filtering tactics for this task. 

Do this without using the Array.prototype.filter method, and you should consider the potential complexity of your solution as the array given may contain more than thousands of entries.

output:
Here is a solution in JavaScript using a simple for-loop and an if-statement to exclude certain occupations:

```js
const people = [
	{ name: "Alice", occupation: "Software Developer" },
	{ name: "Eve", occupation: "Data Scientist" },
	{ name: "Carol", occupation: "Web Designer" },
	{ name: "Bob", occupation: "Quality Analyst" }
];

const excludeOccupation = "Data Scientist";

for (let i = 0; i < people.length; i++) {
    if (people[i].occupation !== excludeOccupation) {
        console.log(people[i].name);
    }
}
```

In the code, we simply iterate through each person in the array and check their occupation. If their occupation is not the one we want to exclude ("Data Scientist" in this case), we log their name to the console. This solution works in O(n) time complexity, which should be efficient enough for an array with thousands of entries.

Alternatively, you can use a while-loop if you feel more comfortable with that:

```js
let i = 0;
while (i < people.length) {
    if (people[i].occupation !== excludeOccupation) {
        console.log(people[i].name);
    }
    i++;
}
```

Both versions do the same thing and have the same time complexity, so the best one to use would depend on your personal preference or the coding style guide you are following.

