Here is the custom Linked List implementation in Java that includes the "hashCode", "equals" and "toString" methods:

```java
public class CustomLinkedList<T> {
    Node<T> head;

    static class Node<T> {
        T data;
        Node<T> next;

        Node(T data) {
            this.data = data;
            this.next = null;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;

        CustomLinkedList<?> that = (CustomLinkedList<?>) o;

        Node<T> currentListNode = this.head;
        Node<?> thatListNode = that.head;

        while(currentListNode != null || thatListNode != null) {
            if(currentListNode == null || thatListNode == null) return false;
            if(currentListNode.data != thatListNode.data) return false;

            currentListNode = currentListNode.next;
            thatListNode = thatListNode.next;
        }

        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        int result = 1;

        for (Node<T> x = head; x != null; x = x.next)
            result = 31 * result + (x.data == null ? 0 : x.data.hashCode());

        return result;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        Node<T> temp = head;
        while (temp != null) {
            sb.append(temp.data.toString());
            temp = temp.next;
            if (temp != null) {
                sb.append(" -> ");
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

**Explanation of the code design:**

- This custom LinkedList uses a static inner Node class to represent the nodes in the list. Each Node contains data of generic type T and reference to the next node.

- The `equals` method works by first checking if object o is reference-equal to the current object, and comparing their classes. If any of these checks fails, then they are not equal. Afterwards, it traverses nodes of both lists and checks whether data of each corresponding node in two lists is equal. If encounter a node that is null in one list but not the other, or if the data of two nodes are not the same, return false indicating they are not equal.

- The `hashCode` method generates a unique integer based on the data in the list. The hash is computed by assigning a prime number (31), as a multiplier, to the running total, adding the next node's hashcode (or 0 if the node's value is null) and then continuing from the front of the list.

- The `toString` method converts the content of the list into a string. By traversing through the list, it appends each non-null node’s data to a StringBuilder. It also adds an arrow (->) after every node, except for the last one. Then it returns the string representation of the entire list nodes.

