Here is a Java program that generates random passwords using the java.util.Random and java.security.SecureRandom classes. This program complies with the basic rule of having at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, and one number.

```java
import java.util.Random;
import java.security.SecureRandom;

public class Main {
    // Possible password characters.
    static final String CHARS_LOWER = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
    static final String CHARS_UPPER = CHARS_LOWER.toUpperCase();
    static final String NUMBERS = "0123456789";
    static final String ALL_CHARS = CHARS_LOWER + CHARS_UPPER + NUMBERS;

    static Random random = new SecureRandom();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Random Password: " + generateRandomPassword(8));
    }

    public static String generateRandomPassword(int length) {
        String password = "";
        // Ensure the password has at least one digit, one lower and one upper case letter
        password += randomCharacter(CHARS_LOWER);
        password += randomCharacter(CHARS_UPPER);
        password += randomCharacter(NUMBERS);

        // Fill the remaining with random characters from all
        for (int i = 3; i < length; i++) {
            password += randomCharacter(ALL_CHARS);
        }

        // Shuffle it to eliminate predictability in password structure
        return shuffleString(password);
    }

    public static char randomCharacter(String characters) {
        int index = random.nextInt(characters.length());
        return characters.charAt(index);
    }

    // Function to shuffle a string
    public static String shuffleString(String string) {
        char[] chars = string.toCharArray();
        for (int i = 0; i < chars.length; i++) {
            int randomIndex = random.nextInt(chars.length);
            char temp = chars[i];
            chars[i] = chars[randomIndex];
            chars[randomIndex] = temp;
        }
        return new String(chars);
    }
}
```

