Here is a simple Java program that accomplishes this. It prompts user to input the number, source base, and target base, and then converts the number from the source base to the target base.

This program takes care of error handling for invalid input numbers and base values via try-catch blocks and conditions. Values outside the range of Integer are treated as signed overflow. This solution does not treat any numbers as signed and does not provide for negative number conversions.

```Java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter the number you want to convert: ");
        String inputValue = scanner.next();

        System.out.println("Enter the source base (from 2 to 36): ");
        int sourceBase = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.println("Enter the target base (from 2 to 36): ");
        int targetBase = scanner.nextInt();

        try {
            if (sourceBase < 2 || sourceBase > 36 || targetBase < 2 || targetBase > 36) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Base value is out of range. It should be between 2 and 36.");
            }

            // Convert to decimal base as intermediate step
            int decimalValue = Integer.parseInt(inputValue, sourceBase);

            // Convert fromdecimal to the target base
            String outputValue = Integer.toString(decimalValue, targetBase);

            System.out.printf("The number %s in base %d equals %s in base %d.", inputValue, sourceBase, outputValue.toUpperCase(), targetBase);

        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! The input is not an unsigned number in the source base.");

        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

Please note that this solution does not handle negative inputs, as negative numbers are generally signed values. Depending on the behavior you want, you may need to adjust this code accordingly to handle negative values. Conversion of signed numbers from one base to another is ambiguous and requires a clear specification, as the ways to represent negative numbers (like 2's complement, 1's complement, sign and magnitude) vary and depend on the context/specific computing system.

