Create a function that calculates the modulo without using any built-in modulo or division operators (like '/' or '%'). It should return the remainder when an integer n is divided by another integer m.

```cpp
int modulo(int n, int m)
{
    return n / m;
}
```

output:
Your current implementation is wrong as it uses the division operator instead of finding the remainder when 'n' is divided by 'm'. Here is a correct implementation of your function using only subtraction:

```cpp
int modulo(int n, int m) {

    if (m == 0) {
        return -1; // Return error if m is 0 because you can't divide by 0
    }

    // If n is less than m then n is the remainder
    if (n < m) {
        return n;
    }

    while (n >= m) {
        n -= m;
    }

    return n;
}
```

This function will repeatedly subtract 'm' from 'n' until 'n' is less than 'm'. At that point, 'n' will be the remainder when originally 'n' is divided by 'm'. It also handles the case where 'm' is '0', which is not allowed in division operations. 

For example -
modulo(10, 3) will return 1, 
modulo(18, 5) will return 3, and 
modulo(7, 0) will return -1.

To call an API using a Node.js Promise, you would typically use a library like "Axios" or the built-in "https" module. Below is an example using the Axios library. Please make sure to install Axios library to your project using npm.

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

function fetchDataFromAPI() {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    axios.get('https://api.example.com/data', {
      params: {
        param: 'value'
      }
    })
    .then(response => {
      resolve(response.data);
    })
    .catch(error => {
      reject(error);
    });
  });
}

fetchDataFromAPI().then(data => {
  console.log(data);
}).catch(error => {
  console.error(error);
});
```

Here is how you can achieve it using the built-in "https" module.

```javascript
const https = require('https');

function fetchDataFromAPI() {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    https.get('https://api.example.com/data?param=value', (res) => {
      let data = '';

      // A chunk of data has been recieved.
      res.on('data', (chunk) => {
        data += chunk;
      });

      // The whole response has been received.
      res.on('end', () => {
        resolve(JSON.parse(data));
      });

    }).on("error", (err) => {
      reject(err);
    });
  });
}

fetchDataFromAPI().then(data => {
  console.log(data);
}).catch(error => {
  console.error(error);
});
```

Both these examples will fetch data from `https://api.example.com/data?param=value` and log it to the console. In case of an error, the error will be caught and logged to the console.

