import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main{
    public static void countChars(String input) {
        int[] asciiCount = new int[128]; // ASCII range
        input = input.toLowerCase(); // making input case-insensitive

        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); ++i) 
            asciiCount[(int)input.charAt(i)]++;

        for (int i = 0; i < 128; ++i)  
            if (asciiCount[i] > 0)
                System.out.println((char)i + ": " + asciiCount[i]);
    }

    public static void main(String args[]){
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter a String of characters:");
        String text = input.nextLine();
        countChars(text);
    }
}
```

To try the program, compile and run it in your Java environment, then when prompted, enter any string of characters. The output will print the frequency of each unique character in the string.

This program works by creating an array of integers with a size of 128 to cover all ASCII characters. It then increments the frequency of the respective index of the character converted to its ASCII value in the array. After that, it just goes through all the indices and prints the character and the frequency. 

It handles the case-insensitivity by converting all the input characters to lower case before processing.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n). It only reads through all the characters in the string once for counting, and another time for printing the results. Due to the array usage of an independent, small size (128), the space complexity is considered to be O(1) - constant. This will make it scale well for large inputs.

