Exception handling in Java is achieved through five keywords: `try`, `catch`, `throw`, `throws`, and `finally`.

Here's the general form of an exception-handling block:

```java
try {
  // Block of code to monitor for errors
} catch (ExceptionType1 e1) {
  // Block of code to handle ExceptionType1
} catch (ExceptionType2 e2) {
  // Block of code to handle ExceptionType2
} finally {
  // Block of code to be always executed whether an exception occurred or not
}
```

1. `try`: This block contains set of statements where an exception can occur. It is always followed by "catch" or "finally" block(s). If an exception occurs in the try block, that exception is handled by the catch block associated with it.

2. `catch`: This block contains the code to handle an exception. It must be used after the try block only. You can use multiple catch blocks with a single try.

```java
try {
  // Code that may raise an exception
} catch (ExceptionType name) {
  // Handle the exception
}
```

3. `throw`: It is used to explicitly throw an exception from a method or any block of code. We can throw either checked or unchecked exception.

```java
throw instance;
```

4. `throws`: It is used to declare an exception. It provides information to the caller of the method about the exception.

```java
Type methodName() throws ExceptionType {
  // Code
}
```

5. `finally`: This block is used to execute the important code of the program. It is executed whether an exception is handled or not. For example, if you open a file it must be closed whether an exception is raised or not.

```java
try {
  // Code that may raise an exception
} catch (ExceptionType name) {
  // Handle the exception
} finally {
  // Execute regardless of an Exception
}
```

Here's an example of how exception handling works in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int[] array = new int[5];
            System.out.println(array[7]);
        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            System.out.println("Exception thrown :" + e);
        } finally {
            System.out.println("This is finally block always runs!");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, trying to access `array[7]` when the array size is 5, throws an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException which is then caught and handled in the catch block. The finally block executes regardless of whether an exception was thrown or not.

