<script>
    // Wrap in a function to capture variables
    function handleResize() {
        // Access the canvas element
        var canvas = document.getElementById('myCanvas');

        // Calculate and apply 70% width
        var windowWidth = window.innerWidth;
        var canvasWidth = windowWidth * 0.7; // 70%
        canvas.width = canvasWidth;

        // Apply minimum height of 300px
        var windowHeight = window.innerHeight;
        var canvasHeight = Math.max(windowHeight, 300); // Ensure minimum 300px
        canvas.height = canvasHeight;
    }

    // Debounce the resize event handler to limit CPU usage
    var resizeTimeout;
    window.addEventListener('resize', function(event) {
        clearTimeout(resizeTimeout);
        resizeTimeout = setTimeout(handleResize, 100);
    });

    // Call manually the first time to set initial size
    handleResize();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

This HTML file includes an inline JavaScript code block that first declares the `handleResize` function to dynamically adjust the canvas size. The size adjustment is made by setting the `width` and `height` properties of the canvas element according to the size of the browser window.

The script then attaches a 'resize' event listener to the window object. Whenever this event is fired, the `handleResize` function is triggered through a `setTimeout` function, providing a debounce effect to limit the number of times the `handleResize` function is called in rapid succession.

Finally, the `handleResize` function is manually called once to ensure the canvas size is correctly set when the page is initially loaded.

