Sure, here is a simple solution using Node.js to reverse a string and to determine if the string is a palindrome after ignoring punctuations, spaces and case sensitivity.

```javascript
function reverseStringAndCheckPalindrome(str) {
  let cleanedStr = str.toLowerCase().replace(/[\W_]/g, '');  // \W matches any non-word character and _ is for underscore. 
  // g makes it global
  let reversedStr = '';

  for(let i = cleanedStr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    reversedStr += cleanedStr[i];
  }

  if(cleanedStr === reversedStr) {
    return `Input string "${str}" is a palindrome and its reversed string is "${reversedStr}"`;
  }else {
    return `The reversed string of "${str}" is "${reversedStr}"`;
  }  
}

console.log(reverseStringAndCheckPalindrome('A man, a plan, a canal: Panama'));
console.log(reverseStringAndCheckPalindrome('Hello World'));
```

First the function lowercases the whole string and then replaces all non-word characters and underscores with empty strings which leaves us with a string of characters only, this is done to remove all the special characters, punctuation and spaces.

Next it goes through the cleaned string backwards appending each character to the `reversedStr` variable.

Finally, it checks if the cleaned string is equal to the reversed string, if yes then it's a palindrome so a respective string is returned, otherwise a different string is returned.

Here's a simple C++ code to compute the transpose of a given 2D matrix. This code works by switching the row and column indices for each element in the original matrix to get the transformed matrix:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int matrix[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
    int transpose[3][3];

    // Finding transpose of the matrix
    for(int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for(int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            transpose[i][j] = matrix[j][i];
        }
    }

    // Displaying the transpose of the matrix
    cout << "Transpose of the matrix: " << endl;
    for(int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for(int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            cout << transpose[i][j] << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

