Sure! To explain, AJAX long-polling is a technique that's used to simulate a real-time connection with a server for use cases where a real-time data transfer is required, such as a chat application or live updates. This technique requires a bit of work to set up, but the results can be quite powerful. 

Here is a simple example of an AJAX long polling set up. This will be a single page application that pings the server every 5 seconds to get an update. 

Step One: Create the HTML file
First, let's create the basic HTML layout.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>AJAX Long Polling Tutorial</title>
</head>
<body>
  <p id="response">Waiting for server response...</p>
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

Step Two: Create the JavaScript file.
In the script.js file:

```javascript
(function poll(){
    var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest(); // Create a new XMLHttpRequest object

    xhr.open('GET', 'http://localhost:8888', true); // Prepare the request
    xhr.onreadystatechange = function () {
        if (xhr.readyState == 4) { // If the HTTP request has completed
            if (xhr.status == 200) { // If the HTTP response code is 200 (OK)
                document.getElementById('response').innerHTML = xhr.responseText;
            }
           // else handle error, and re-poll
        }
    };

    xhr.onerror = function() {
    // handle error here, a timeout or disconnection, by restarting the poll
     setTimeout(poll, 2000);
    }

    xhr.send(null); // Send the request now
})();
```

This JavaScript code:
- sets up an anonymous function (to avoid polluting the global namespace) that is immediately executed.
- Then it creates a new XMLHttpRequest object.
- opens a new HTTP request to the server
- Once gets a server response, it checks the HTTP response code.
    - if it's 200, it means everything is OK so updates the #response element's content with the response text
    - if it's not 200, it means there's been an error so the poll function is called again to try another request
- If an error occurred while making the request or on server disconnection, onerror is triggered, the poll function is called again after 2 seconds
- sends the HTTP request

Please make sure to adjust the address ('http://localhost:8888') according to your server details.

Step Three: Create the server file (optional)
For this tutorial, I'll assume you're running a simple Node.js server. Here's an example of a server that would work (server.js):

```javascript
var http = require('http'), //include the http module
    fs = require('fs'); //include the filesystem module

http.createServer(function (req, res) { //create the server
    setTimeout(function () { //simulate a long request by adding a delay
        res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/plain'});
        res.end('Hello World\n');
    }, 5000);   
}).listen(8888, '127.0.0.1');

console.log('Waiting for requests...');
```

You can change 5000 to another number for simulating the delay. 

Don't forget to start your node server : `node server.js`

This is a very basic example but should give you a good idea of how this technique works. Note that in a more advanced scenario, you would also include error handling for the case where the HTTP request fails. For example, you could set up the code to automatically retry the request until it succeeds.

