Given a piece of jQuery code utilizing the fadeIn() function as below, identify the error, explain the correct usage of the fadeIn() function and the impact of different parameters used in it. Additionally, refactor the code to improve its performance and discuss your approach to it.

#Given Code#:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function(){
    $("button").click(function(){
        $("#div1").fadeIn(3000);
        $("#div2").fadeIn("slow");
        $("#div3").fadeIn(500);
        $("#div4").fadeIn(-200);
    });
});
```

output:
The given code uses jQuery's fadeIn() function, which gradually changes the opacity of the selected elements, transforming from hidden to visible. The function's great feature is that it enables us to customize the duration of the effect with a pre-defined string or a number of milliseconds. 

The given code does not seem to have any syntax errors or incorrect usage of the fadeIn() function per se. All usages match the fadeIn()'s parameters documentation, where it takes `'fast'`, `'slow'` or an integer representing milliseconds as input.

However:
"$("#div4").fadeIn(-200);" looks odd because a negative duration for the fadeIn() function might not make sense and it's usually considered as a programming mistake. If a negative number or zero is given, jQuery treats it as if it was zero and completes the animation instantly.

To improve the code, rather than using multiple individual ID selectors which can be slow, you might want to use a common class selector or store them in variables if these elements are re-used elsewhere in code. Here is the refactored code:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function(){
    $("button").click(function(){
        var $div1 = $("#div1");
        var $div2 = $("#div2");
        var $div3 = $("#div3");
        var $div4 = $("#div4");

        $div1.fadeIn(3000);
        $div2.fadeIn("slow");
        $div3.fadeIn(500);
        $div4.fadeIn(0); // Negative duration treated as 0
    });
});
```

We could even further instigate some form of chaining or a callback structure if the fadeIn() animations are to be sequential, not simultaneous:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function(){
    $("button").click(function(){
        var $div1 = $("#div1");
        var $div2 = $("#div2");
        var $div3 = $("#div3");
        var $div4 = $("#div4");

        $div1.fadeIn(3000, function() {
            $div2.fadeIn("slow", function() {
                $div3.fadeIn(500, function() {
                    $div4.fadeIn();
                });
            });
        });
    });
});
```

On the other hand, if the elements to be faded in possess some common characteristics, we might select them all together:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function(){
    $("button").click(function(){
        $(".fadeTarget").fadeIn(500);
    });
});
```
In this case, all DOM elements having the class 'fadeTarget' will fade in simultaneously when the button is clicked. This is obviously way more efficient if many elements are intended to behave in the same way.

In Java, you can implement a Binary Search function for an array of strings as follows:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] arr = {"bar", "baz", "foo", "quux"};
        String target = "foo";

        int result = binarySearch(arr, target);

        if (result == -1) 
            System.out.println("Target not present in array");
        else 
            System.out.println("Target found at index " + result);
    }

    public static int binarySearch(String[] arr, String target) {
        int l = 0, r = arr.length - 1;
        while (l <= r) {
            int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;

            int res = target.compareTo(arr[mid]);

            // Check if target is present at the mid index
            if (res == 0) 
                return mid;

            // If target greater, ignore left half
            if (res > 0) 
                l = mid + 1;

            // If target is smaller, ignore right half
            else 
                r = mid - 1;
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Please note that for binary search to work, the array should be sorted. If the array is not sorted, then we should sort it first before performing the binary search. In the given example, the array is sorted in ascending order.

Binary search works by dividing the array into two halves about the middle element and then checking if the target element is less than, greater than or equal to the middle element. If the target is equal to the middle element, then the index of the middle element is returned. If the target is less than the middle element, then the right half of the array is ignored and the same process is applied to the left half. If the target is greater than the middle element, then the left half of the array is ignored and the same process is applied to the right half. This process repeats until the target element is found or until the entire array has been searched.

