Here is a simple C++ function that prints the Fibonacci sequence up to a given number n.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void printFibonacci(int n){
   static int n1=0, n2=1, n3;

   // base case
   if(n>0){
      n3 = n1 + n2;
      n1 = n2;
      n2 = n3;
      cout<<n3<<" ";
      printFibonacci(n-1);
   }
}

int main(){
   int n;
   cout<<"Enter the number of elements: ";
   cin>>n;
   cout<<"Fibonacci Series: ";
   cout<<"0 "<<"1 ";
   printFibonacci(n-2);  // n-2 because 2 numbers are already printed
   return 0;
}
```

Detailed description of the above code:

The function `printFibonacci` is designed to print out the Fibonacci sequence up to a number specified by the user. The Fibonacci sequence is a series of numbers where a number is the addition of the last two numbers, starting with 0 and 1. 

In this function, we start by defining three static integers `n1`, `n2`, and `n3`. `n1` and `n2` are initialized to the first two numbers of the Fibonacci sequence, 0 and 1, while `n3` is uninitialized at the start.

The function then enters a recursive loop. Within this loop, `n3` is calculated as the sum of `n1` and `n2`, then `n1` and `n2` are updated to the last and the new number of the sequence, respectively. This updated number `n3` is printed to the console. 

The recursion continues until the specified number of terms have been printed. If the requested number of terms is 0 or negative, the function will not start the recursion and nothing is printed.

In the `main` function, we ask the user to enter the number of terms they want to generate from the Fibonacci sequence. We then print the first two terms, which are always 0 and 1, and call our function with `n-2` as its argument (since we already printed two terms). The Fibonacci series of the requested length is then displayed on the console.

