Here is a solution using JavaScript. It mainly uses regex for validation. It assumes the manufacturer info is from the first three octets of the MAC address, which is a simplification because the exact process involves an Organizationally Unique Identifier (OUI) lookup.

```javascript
function validateAndCategorizeMac(mac) {
  // Check if address is MAC-48 or EUI-64
  var validMac48 = /^([0-9A-Fa-f]{2}[:-]){5}([0-9A-Fa-f]{2})$/;
  var validEui64 = /^([0-9A-Fa-f]{2}[:-]){7}([0-9A-Fa-f]{2})$/;

  if (validMac48.test(mac)) {
    console.log(`MAC Address ${mac} is valid and follows MAC-48 format.`);
  } else if (validEui64.test(mac)) {
    console.log(`MAC Address ${mac} is valid and follows EUI-64 format.`);
  } else {
    console.log(`MAC Address ${mac} is not valid.`);
    return;
  }

  // Determine manufacturer info (usually the first three octets)
  var manufacturerInfo = mac.split(':').slice(0,3).join(':');
  console.log(`Manufacturer Info: ${manufacturerInfo}`);

  // Unicast or Multicast can be decided by the least significant bit of the first octet
  var unicastOrMulticast = parseInt(mac.split(':')[0], 16) & 1 ? 'Multicast' : 'Unicast';
  console.log(`Type: ${unicastOrMulticast}`);
}

// Test with some MAC addresses
validateAndCategorizeMac('01:23:45:67:89:ab');
validateAndCategorizeMac('01:23:45:67:89:ab:cd:ef');
validateAndCategorizeMac('01:23:45:67:gh');
```
This script will test if the MAC address is MAC-48 or EUI-64. If the address fails both tests, then it must be invalid. Then it extracts the manufacturer info from the first three octets. It also checks if the address is unicast or multicast by checking the least significant bit of the first octet.

Note: This solution provides a simplified way to find a "manufacturer". In reality, to find the exact manufacturer, you would need to do an Organizationally Unique Identifier (OUI) lookup, which cannot be done without resorting to an external database, API, or library.

