Here is a JavaScript program refactored to use the map() and filter() methods of ES6:

```javascript
let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

if (numbers.length > 1000000) {
    console.error('Array length limit exceeded');
} else {
    let doubleEvenNumbers = numbers
        .filter(number => number % 2 === 0)
        .map(number => number * 2);
}
```
In this refactored solution, no 'for' loop is used. Instead, the ES6 filter() method is used to select only the even elements from the array, and then these selected elements are multiplied by 2 using the map() method. Also, an error message is outputted if the array length exceeds 1 million records.

However, this solution does not yet address the feature that prevents the program from duplicating any processing on the numbers that have already been processed. Let's solve this need with the addition of a Set data structure which only stores unique values:

```javascript
let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

let processedNumbers = new Set();

if (numbers.length > 1000000) {
    console.error('Array length limit exceeded');
} else {
    let doubleEvenNumbers = numbers
        .filter((number) => {
            if (number % 2 === 0 && !processedNumbers.has(number)){
                processedNumbers.add(number);
                return true;
            }
            return false;
        })
        .map((number) => number * 2);
}
```

In this code, a Set 'processedNumbers' is used to hold the already processed numbers. When filtering the 'numbers' for even values, it also checks if the number has already been processed. If not, it adds it to 'processedNumbers' and includes it in the filter.

Please note: the scripts stop processing if the input array exceeds 1 million records but once the operations have been triggered, there is no additional checking mechanism to stop the processing after a million records are produced. This is due to JavaScript's synchronous nature and that there's no built-in way to preemptively stop map or filter methods once it runs.

To create a table and allow sorting in HTML, it can be done with JavaScript. 

Before the solution, we need to understand that HTML will be used to create a layout for the web page while JavaScript will be adding the sorting function to it.

Here is a simple JavaScript function that you can use to make your table sortable:

```javascript
function sortTable(n) {
  var table, rows, switching, i, x, y, shouldSwitch, dir, switchcount = 0;
  table = document.getElementById("myTable");
  switching = true;
  dir = "asc";
  while (switching) {
    switching = false;
    rows = table.rows;
    for (i = 1; i < (rows.length - 1); i++) {
      shouldSwitch = false;
      x = rows[i].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
      y = rows[i + 1].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
      if (dir == "asc") {
        if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() > y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
          shouldSwitch = true;
          break;
        }
      } else if (dir == "desc") {
        if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() < y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
          shouldSwitch = true;
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    if (shouldSwitch) {
      rows[i].parentNode.insertBefore(rows[i + 1], rows[i]);
      switching = true;
      switchcount ++;
    } else {
      if (switchcount == 0 && dir == "asc") {
        dir = "desc";
        switching = true;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

